DSP.mod(2,{"m2-checklist":{"id":"m2-checklist","slug":"sql-topic-checklist","m":2,"title":"SQL topic checklist","min":20,"type":"learn","priority":"Highest","prereq":[],"tags":["sql","fundamentals"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"The SQL topics interviews assume, taught with small verified examples: filtering, aggregation, NULL behaviour, joins, set operations, subqueries and CTEs.","keyTerms":["SELECT","WHERE","GROUP BY","HAVING","NULL","JOIN","CTE","window functions","subquery","UNION"],"interviewQs":["What is the difference between WHERE and HAVING?","What do COUNT(*), COUNT(col) and COUNT(DISTINCT col) return?","Why does NOT IN return no rows when the subquery contains NULL?","When would you use EXISTS instead of IN?"],"mistakes":["Treating NULL as a value (col = NULL)","Using NOT IN with a nullable subquery","Counting rows when you need distinct users"],"practice":"Work through every bullet of the checklist on the practice schema and write one query per bullet.","related":["m2-schema","m2-execution-order","m2-ranking"],"points":"Fundamentals\n\n- SELECT, WHERE, ORDER BY, LIMIT/TOP, DISTINCT\n- LIKE, IN, BETWEEN, IS NULL, CASE WHEN\n- Aggregates: COUNT, SUM, AVG, MIN, MAX; COUNT(*) vs COUNT(col) vs COUNT(DISTINCT col)\n- GROUP BY, HAVING (WHERE vs HAVING)\n- NULL behavior: NULL = NULL is unknown; aggregates ignore NULLs; COALESCE, NULLIF\n\nJoins and set operations\n\n- INNER, LEFT, RIGHT, FULL OUTER, CROSS, SELF\n- Anti-join (NOT EXISTS / LEFT JOIN ... IS NULL) and semi-join (EXISTS)\n- UNION vs UNION ALL, INTERSECT, EXCEPT/MINUS\n- Join on multiple keys, non-equi joins, fan-out/duplication pitfalls\n\nAdvanced\n\n- Subqueries (scalar, correlated, in FROM)\n- CTEs, multiple CTEs, recursive CTEs (basic)\n- Window functions: ROW_NUMBER, RANK, DENSE_RANK, NTILE, LAG, LEAD, FIRST_VALUE, LAST_VALUE, SUM() OVER, AVG() OVER, frames (ROWS BETWEEN ...)\n- Date/time: DATE_TRUNC, EXTRACT, DATEDIFF/DATE_PART, interval arithmetic\n- String functions: SUBSTRING, CONCAT, SPLIT_PART, REGEXP\n- Pivoting with CASE WHEN, conditional aggregation\n- Indexes, primary/foreign keys, normalization (1NF-3NF), ACID, OLTP vs OLAP"},"intuitive":"<p>SQL is how you ask a big spreadsheet-like database questions. You say which columns you want (SELECT), from which table (FROM), keeping only some rows (WHERE), then you can group rows into piles (GROUP BY) and summarise each pile (COUNT, SUM, AVG). A join is stapling two tables together where they share an id. The one weird rule to remember: a missing value (NULL) means &quot;I don&#39;t know&quot;, so even &quot;I don&#39;t know equals I don&#39;t know&quot; is not true, it is also &quot;I don&#39;t know&quot;.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> SQL is the biggest single filter in data science interviews, and most mistakes come from NULLs and joins.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Filter, group, join, and respect NULL.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;SQL asks a database questions. SELECT chooses columns, FROM the table, WHERE keeps rows, GROUP BY makes piles and aggregates like COUNT, SUM and AVG summarise each pile, HAVING filters piles. Joins combine tables on a shared key: inner keeps matches, left keeps every left row and fills NULLs. The rule that trips people up is NULL: it means unknown, so NULL equals NULL is not true, aggregates skip NULLs, and NOT IN with a NULL in the list returns nothing. COUNT star counts rows, COUNT of a column counts non-NULL values. The catch: check the grain of each table before joining, or sums get inflated.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"A LEFT JOIN and COUNT, traced on real rows","html":"<p>Using the practice data (topic 2.4): how many orders does each of users 6, 7 and 8 have, including users with none?</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> u.user_id, <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">COUNT</span>(<span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> rows_seen, <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">COUNT</span>(o.order_id) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> orders\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> users u\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">LEFT</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">JOIN</span> orders o <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ON</span> o.user_id <span class=\"hljs-operator\">=</span> u.user_id\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> u.user_id <span class=\"hljs-operator\">&gt;=</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">6</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> u.user_id;</code></pre></div>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>FROM users:</strong> start with users 6, 7 and 8.</li>\n<li><strong>LEFT JOIN orders:</strong> keep every user. Users with orders get one row per order; user 8 has no orders, so he gets one row with NULLs on the right.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>user_id</th><th>order_id</th><th>amount</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>6</td><td>10</td><td>700</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>15</td><td>500</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>16</td><td>1400</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>NULL</td><td>NULL</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ol start=\"3\">\n<li><strong>GROUP BY user_id:</strong> three piles: user 6 (2 rows), user 7 (1 row), user 8 (1 row).</li>\n<li><strong>Count two ways.</strong> <code>COUNT(*)</code> counts rows: 2, 1, <strong>1</strong>. <code>COUNT(o.order_id)</code> skips NULLs: 2, 1, <strong>0</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Result:</strong> user 6 has 2 orders, user 7 has 1, user 8 has <strong>0</strong>, which is the right answer only with <code>COUNT(o.order_id)</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> A LEFT JOIN keeps unmatched rows by filling NULLs. <code>COUNT(*)</code> would report one order for a user who never ordered. Count a column from the right-hand table.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-select-where-order-by-limit-distinct-case","text":"SELECT, WHERE, ORDER BY, LIMIT, DISTINCT, CASE","level":3},{"id":"h-null-the-source-of-most-mistakes","text":"NULL: the source of most mistakes","level":3},{"id":"h-group-by-and-having","text":"GROUP BY and HAVING","level":3},{"id":"h-joins-in-one-picture","text":"Joins, in one picture","level":3},{"id":"h-set-operations","text":"Set operations","level":3},{"id":"h-subqueries-and-ctes","text":"Subqueries and CTEs","level":3},{"id":"h-window-functions-in-one-sentence","text":"Window functions in one sentence","level":3},{"id":"h-dates-strings-pivots","text":"Dates, strings, pivots","level":3},{"id":"h-keys-indexes-normal-forms-acid","text":"Keys, indexes, normal forms, ACID","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p><strong>Fundamentals</strong></p>\n"},{"k":"cl","id":"m2-checklist.c1","kind":"list","items":["SELECT, WHERE, ORDER BY, LIMIT/TOP, DISTINCT","LIKE, IN, BETWEEN, IS NULL, CASE WHEN","Aggregates: COUNT, SUM, AVG, MIN, MAX; <code>COUNT(*)</code> vs <code>COUNT(col)</code> vs <code>COUNT(DISTINCT col)</code>","GROUP BY, HAVING (WHERE vs HAVING)","NULL behavior: <code>NULL = NULL</code> is unknown; aggregates ignore NULLs; <code>COALESCE</code>, <code>NULLIF</code>"]},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p><strong>Joins and set operations</strong></p>\n"},{"k":"cl","id":"m2-checklist.c2","kind":"list","items":["INNER, LEFT, RIGHT, FULL OUTER, CROSS, SELF","Anti-join (NOT EXISTS / LEFT JOIN ... IS NULL) and semi-join (EXISTS)","UNION vs UNION ALL, INTERSECT, EXCEPT/MINUS","Join on multiple keys, non-equi joins, fan-out/duplication pitfalls"]},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p><strong>Advanced</strong></p>\n"},{"k":"cl","id":"m2-checklist.c3","kind":"list","items":["Subqueries (scalar, correlated, in FROM)","CTEs, multiple CTEs, recursive CTEs (basic)","Window functions: <code>ROW_NUMBER</code>, <code>RANK</code>, <code>DENSE_RANK</code>, <code>NTILE</code>, <code>LAG</code>, <code>LEAD</code>, <code>FIRST_VALUE</code>, <code>LAST_VALUE</code>, <code>SUM() OVER</code>, <code>AVG() OVER</code>, frames (<code>ROWS BETWEEN ...</code>)","Date/time: <code>DATE_TRUNC</code>, <code>EXTRACT</code>, <code>DATEDIFF</code>/<code>DATE_PART</code>, interval arithmetic","String functions: <code>SUBSTRING</code>, <code>CONCAT</code>, <code>SPLIT_PART</code>, <code>REGEXP</code>","Pivoting with <code>CASE WHEN</code>, conditional aggregation","Indexes, primary/foreign keys, normalization (1NF-3NF), ACID, OLTP vs OLAP"]},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<p>The checklist is a map; this page is a tour of it on the tiny practice data (see <em>Practice schema</em> for the script). Every result below was run on that data.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-select-where-order-by-limit-distinct-case\">SELECT, WHERE, ORDER BY, LIMIT, DISTINCT, CASE</h3>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> name, salary,\n       <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">CASE</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHEN</span> salary <span class=\"hljs-operator\">&gt;=</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">80000</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">THEN</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;high&#x27;</span>\n            <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHEN</span> salary <span class=\"hljs-operator\">&gt;=</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">60000</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">THEN</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;mid&#x27;</span>\n            <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ELSE</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;low&#x27;</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">END</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> band\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> employees\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> salary <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">DESC</span>\nLIMIT <span class=\"hljs-number\">4</span>;</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>name</th><th>salary</th><th>band</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Zoya</td><td>95000</td><td>high</td></tr><tr><td>Asha</td><td>90000</td><td>high</td></tr><tr><td>Isha</td><td>80000</td><td>high</td></tr><tr><td>Ravi</td><td>70000</td><td>mid</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Other filters to know: <code>col LIKE &#39;A%&#39;</code> (pattern), <code>col IN (1, 2)</code>, <code>col BETWEEN 10 AND 20</code> (inclusive), <code>col IS NULL</code>. <code>LIMIT n</code> is <code>TOP n</code> in SQL Server and <code>FETCH FIRST n ROWS ONLY</code> in standard SQL and Oracle.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-null-the-source-of-most-mistakes\">NULL: the source of most mistakes</h3>\n<p>NULL means <em>unknown</em>. Any comparison with NULL is unknown, not true: <code>NULL = NULL</code> is unknown, and <code>WHERE</code> keeps only rows where the condition is true.</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">COUNT</span>(<span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span>)                   <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> all_rows,      <span class=\"hljs-comment\">-- 9</span>\n       <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">COUNT</span>(manager_id)          <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> non_null,      <span class=\"hljs-comment\">-- 8  (Asha has no manager)</span>\n       <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">COUNT</span>(<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">DISTINCT</span> manager_id) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> distinct_mgr   <span class=\"hljs-comment\">-- 4</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> employees;</code></pre></div>\n<ul>\n<li><code>COUNT(*)</code> counts rows. <code>COUNT(col)</code> counts non-NULL values. <code>COUNT(DISTINCT col)</code> counts distinct non-NULL values.</li>\n<li>Aggregates other than <code>COUNT(*)</code> <strong>ignore</strong> NULLs: <code>AVG</code> divides by the number of non-NULL values, not the number of rows.</li>\n<li>Handle NULL with <code>COALESCE(x, 0)</code> (first non-NULL) and <code>NULLIF(a, b)</code> (NULL when a = b; classic divide-by-zero guard: <code>x / NULLIF(y, 0)</code>).</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<aside class=\"callout trap\"><div class=\"callout-t\">NOT IN and NULL</div><div class=\"callout-b\"><p>&quot;Employees who manage nobody&quot; sounds easy:</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> name <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> employees\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> emp_id <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">NOT</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">IN</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> manager_id <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> employees);   <span class=\"hljs-comment\">-- returns NO rows</span></code></pre></div>\n<p>The subquery contains a NULL (Asha&#39;s <code>manager_id</code>), so every <code>NOT IN</code> test evaluates to unknown. Safe versions:</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> emp_id <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">NOT</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">IN</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> manager_id <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> employees <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> manager_id <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">IS</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">NOT NULL</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\">-- or, best:</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">NOT</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">EXISTS</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> employees x <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> x.manager_id <span class=\"hljs-operator\">=</span> e.emp_id)</code></pre></div>\n<p>Both return Meera, Karan, Dev, Nina, Zoya.</p>\n</div></aside>"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-group-by-and-having\">GROUP BY and HAVING</h3>\n<p><code>WHERE</code> filters <strong>rows before</strong> grouping; <code>HAVING</code> filters <strong>groups after</strong> aggregation.</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> d.dept_name, <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">AVG</span>(e.salary) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> avg_sal\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> employees e <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">JOIN</span> departments d <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">USING</span> (dept_id)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> e.hire_date <span class=\"hljs-operator\">&gt;=</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2020-01-01&#x27;</span>          <span class=\"hljs-comment\">-- row filter (before grouping)</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> d.dept_name\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">HAVING</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">AVG</span>(e.salary) <span class=\"hljs-operator\">&gt;</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">65000</span>;              <span class=\"hljs-comment\">-- group filter (after aggregating)</span></code></pre></div>\n<p>Every selected column must be either in <code>GROUP BY</code> or inside an aggregate.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-joins-in-one-picture\">Joins, in one picture</h3>\n<p>Use the interactive demo below to see what each join type keeps.</p>\n"},{"k":"w","src":"a","name":"joins","props":{}},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Join</th><th>Keeps</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>INNER JOIN</code></td><td>rows with a match on both sides</td></tr><tr><td><code>LEFT JOIN</code></td><td>all left rows; right columns are NULL where there is no match</td></tr><tr><td><code>RIGHT JOIN</code></td><td>mirror of LEFT</td></tr><tr><td><code>FULL OUTER JOIN</code></td><td>all rows from both sides</td></tr><tr><td><code>CROSS JOIN</code></td><td>every left row paired with every right row (n × m)</td></tr><tr><td>self join</td><td>a table joined to itself, e.g. employee to manager</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Anti-join</strong> (&quot;rows with no match&quot;) and <strong>semi-join</strong> (&quot;rows with at least one match&quot;):</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-comment\">-- anti-join: users who never ordered</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> u.user_id <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> users u\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">LEFT</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">JOIN</span> orders o <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ON</span> o.user_id <span class=\"hljs-operator\">=</span> u.user_id\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> o.order_id <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">IS</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">NULL</span>;                         <span class=\"hljs-comment\">-- user 8</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\">-- semi-join: users who ordered at least once (no duplicates, even with many orders)</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> user_id <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> users u\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">EXISTS</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> orders o <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> o.user_id <span class=\"hljs-operator\">=</span> u.user_id);</code></pre></div>\n<h3 id=\"h-set-operations\">Set operations</h3>\n<p><code>UNION</code> stacks results and removes duplicates; <code>UNION ALL</code> keeps them (and is faster, no sort or hash needed). <code>INTERSECT</code> keeps rows in both; <code>EXCEPT</code> (<code>MINUS</code> in Oracle) keeps rows in the first but not the second. The column counts and types must match.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-subqueries-and-ctes\">Subqueries and CTEs</h3>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-comment\">-- scalar subquery: orders above the average order</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> order_id, amount <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> orders\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> amount <span class=\"hljs-operator\">&gt;</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">AVG</span>(amount) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> orders);\n\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\">-- CTE: the same idea, named and reusable, reads top to bottom</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WITH</span> avg_order <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">AVG</span>(amount) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> a <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> orders)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> order_id, amount <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> orders, avg_order <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> amount <span class=\"hljs-operator\">&gt;</span> avg_order.a;</code></pre></div>\n<p>A <strong>correlated</strong> subquery refers to the outer row and is conceptually re-evaluated per row (the optimiser often flattens it). A <strong>recursive CTE</strong> repeatedly joins to its own previous output (employee hierarchies, Problem P25).</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-window-functions-in-one-sentence\">Window functions in one sentence</h3>\n<p>A window function computes a value for each row using <em>other rows</em> (its window) without collapsing them the way <code>GROUP BY</code> does. The next topics go deep.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-dates-strings-pivots\">Dates, strings, pivots</h3>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">DATE_TRUNC(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;month&#x27;</span>, order_date)              <span class=\"hljs-comment\">-- Postgres: first day of the month</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">EXTRACT</span>(<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">YEAR</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> order_date)                <span class=\"hljs-comment\">-- year number</span>\norder_date <span class=\"hljs-operator\">+</span> <span class=\"hljs-type\">INTERVAL</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;7 days&#x27;</span>               <span class=\"hljs-comment\">-- Postgres date arithmetic</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">SUBSTRING</span>(email <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FOR</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>), CONCAT(a, b), SPLIT_PART(email, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;@&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">SUM</span>(<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">CASE</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHEN</span> category <span class=\"hljs-operator\">=</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Fashion&#x27;</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">THEN</span> amount <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ELSE</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">END</span>)   <span class=\"hljs-comment\">-- pivot / conditional aggregation</span></code></pre></div>\n<h3 id=\"h-keys-indexes-normal-forms-acid\">Keys, indexes, normal forms, ACID</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Primary key</strong>: uniquely identifies a row (unique + not null). <strong>Foreign key</strong>: references another table&#39;s key, enforcing integrity.</li>\n<li>An <strong>index</strong> (usually a B-tree) speeds lookups and range scans at the cost of slower writes and extra storage.</li>\n<li><strong>Normalisation</strong>: 1NF atomic values, 2NF no partial dependency on part of a composite key, 3NF no transitive dependency. <strong>Denormalisation</strong> duplicates data to make analytical reads cheaper.</li>\n<li><strong>ACID</strong>: Atomicity (all or nothing), Consistency (valid state to valid state), Isolation (concurrent transactions do not interfere), Durability (committed data survives crashes).</li>\n<li><strong>OLTP</strong> systems run many small transactions (normalised); <strong>OLAP</strong> systems run large analytical queries (denormalised star schemas).</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m2-checklist~1","q":"A table <code>t(a)</code> contains the values 1, 2, NULL. What do <code>SELECT COUNT(*) FROM t</code>, <code>COUNT(a)</code>, <code>AVG(a)</code> and <code>SELECT * FROM t WHERE a &lt;&gt; 1</code> return?","a":"<p><code>COUNT(*)</code> = 3 rows; <code>COUNT(a)</code> = 2 (NULL skipped); <code>AVG(a)</code> = (1 + 2) / 2 = 1.5 (NULL ignored, not treated as 0); <code>WHERE a &lt;&gt; 1</code> returns only <code>2</code>, because <code>NULL &lt;&gt; 1</code> is unknown and unknown rows are filtered out. To include the NULL row, write <code>WHERE a &lt;&gt; 1 OR a IS NULL</code>.</p>\n","s":"3, 2, 1.5, and only the row with a = 2."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m2-checklist~ca0_1","q":"A table has values 1, 2, NULL. What do COUNT(*), COUNT(col) and AVG(col) return?","a":"<p>3, 2 and 1.5: NULLs are skipped by COUNT(col) and AVG.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m2-checklist~ca0_2","q":"Why does <code>WHERE id NOT IN (SELECT manager_id ...)</code> return nothing when manager_id has a NULL?","a":"<p><code>id &lt;&gt; NULL</code> is unknown for every row, so no row passes. Use NOT EXISTS or filter the NULLs out.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m2-checklist~ca0_3","q":"WHERE versus HAVING?","a":"<p>WHERE filters rows before grouping; HAVING filters groups after aggregation.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m2-checklist~ta1_1","q":"<p>The employees table has 9 rows; only Asha has <code>manager_id = NULL</code> (she is the top boss). Predict: (a) <code>SELECT COUNT(*), COUNT(manager_id) FROM employees</code>; (b) how many rows <code>WHERE manager_id &lt;&gt; 1</code> returns. Asha (1) manages Ravi, Meera, Isha and Omar.</p>\n","a":"<p>(a) 9 and 8: <code>COUNT(manager_id)</code> skips Asha&#39;s NULL. (b) 4 rows: Karan, Dev, Nina and Zoya. The four people managed by Asha fail the test, and Asha&#39;s own row is dropped too, because <code>NULL &lt;&gt; 1</code> is unknown, not true. If you expected 5, you have met the NULL rule: use <code>manager_id &lt;&gt; 1 OR manager_id IS NULL</code> when the NULL row should be kept.</p>\n"}]},"m2-execution-order":{"id":"m2-execution-order","slug":"logical-order-of-execution","m":2,"title":"Logical order of execution","min":15,"type":"learn","priority":"Highest","prereq":["m2-checklist"],"tags":["sql","fundamentals"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"The logical order in which SQL evaluates a query and how it explains alias scope, window-function placement and WHERE vs HAVING.","keyTerms":["logical query processing","FROM","WHERE","GROUP BY","HAVING","SELECT","window functions","ORDER BY","alias scope"],"interviewQs":["In what order does SQL logically execute a query?","Why can't you use a SELECT alias in the WHERE clause?","Why can't you filter on a window function in WHERE, and how do you work around it?"],"mistakes":["Writing queries in the order they are typed and assuming that is the order they run","Filtering on a window function directly","Referring to an alias in HAVING or WHERE"],"practice":"Take any query you wrote and annotate each clause with its logical step number, then explain which names are visible at each step.","related":["m2-checklist","m2-ranking"],"points":"Consequences: you cannot use a SELECT alias in WHERE (in most DBs); window functions cannot be used directly in WHERE (wrap in CTE/subquery)."},"intuitive":"<p>Think of making a fruit salad. First you pick the bowls of fruit you will use (FROM and JOIN). Then you throw away the bad fruit (WHERE). Then you sort the rest into piles (GROUP BY) and maybe throw away small piles (HAVING). Only then do you decide how to label and present each serving (SELECT), tidy up duplicates, arrange them in order (ORDER BY) and take just the first few (LIMIT). You write the recipe starting with &quot;SELECT&quot;, but the kitchen works in the other order.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> &quot;Why does my alias not work here?&quot; becomes easy once you know the kitchen&#39;s order.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> FROM, WHERE, GROUP BY, HAVING, SELECT, DISTINCT, ORDER BY, LIMIT.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;SQL is written in one order and run in another. Logically it goes FROM and joins, then WHERE, then GROUP BY, then HAVING, then SELECT, including window functions, then DISTINCT, ORDER BY and LIMIT. That explains the common errors. A SELECT alias cannot be used in WHERE because WHERE runs first. A window function cannot be in WHERE for the same reason, so I compute it in a CTE and filter outside. ORDER BY can use an alias because it runs after SELECT. WHERE filters rows early and cheaply; HAVING filters aggregated groups. The catch: this is the logical order; the optimiser may physically reorder work as long as the result is identical.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Follow one query through the pipeline","html":"<p>Question: among users with at least two delivered orders before March, who spent the most?</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> user_id, <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">COUNT</span>(<span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> n, <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">SUM</span>(amount) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> spend\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> orders\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> status <span class=\"hljs-operator\">=</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;delivered&#x27;</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AND</span> order_date <span class=\"hljs-operator\">&lt;</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-03-01&#x27;</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> user_id\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">HAVING</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">COUNT</span>(<span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span>) <span class=\"hljs-operator\">&gt;=</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> spend <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">DESC</span>\nLIMIT <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>;</code></pre></div>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>FROM orders:</strong> all 16 orders.</li>\n<li><strong>WHERE:</strong> keep delivered orders before March. 9 rows survive (order 4 is cancelled; orders 11 to 16 are in March).</li>\n<li><strong>GROUP BY user_id:</strong> six piles.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>user</th><th>orders</th><th>spend</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>3</td><td>2,900</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>2</td><td>3,400</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1</td><td>1,200</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1</td><td>3,000</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>1</td><td>1,800</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>1</td><td>700</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ol start=\"4\">\n<li><strong>HAVING COUNT(*) &gt;= 2:</strong> only users 1 and 2 remain. User 4 spent more than user 1 but has one order, so the group is removed here.</li>\n<li><strong>SELECT:</strong> compute <code>n</code> and <code>spend</code> for the remaining groups.</li>\n<li><strong>ORDER BY spend DESC:</strong> user 2 (3,400), then user 1 (2,900). The alias <code>spend</code> works here because ORDER BY runs after SELECT.</li>\n<li><strong>LIMIT 1:</strong> user 2.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Write the query top to bottom, but read it in this order. If you had written <code>WHERE spend &gt; 1000</code>, it would fail: WHERE runs at step 2, before <code>spend</code> exists.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-the-logical-order-with-what-each-step-can-see","text":"The logical order, with what each step can see","level":3},{"id":"h-three-consequences-you-will-hit-constantly","text":"Three consequences you will hit constantly","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Text</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">FROM / JOIN  -&gt;  WHERE  -&gt;  GROUP BY  -&gt;  HAVING  -&gt;  SELECT (incl. window functions)\n-&gt; DISTINCT  -&gt;  ORDER BY  -&gt;  LIMIT</code></pre></div>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p>Consequences: you cannot use a SELECT alias in WHERE (in most DBs); window functions cannot be used directly in WHERE (wrap in CTE/subquery).</p>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-the-logical-order-with-what-each-step-can-see\">The logical order, with what each step can see</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Clause</th><th>What it does</th><th>Can see...</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td><code>FROM</code> / <code>JOIN</code></td><td>Build the working table</td><td>table columns</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td><code>WHERE</code></td><td>Filter <strong>rows</strong></td><td>table columns (not aliases, not aggregates, not windows)</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td><code>GROUP BY</code></td><td>Collapse rows into groups</td><td>table columns</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td><code>HAVING</code></td><td>Filter <strong>groups</strong></td><td>aggregates</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td><code>SELECT</code></td><td>Compute output columns, <strong>including window functions</strong></td><td>everything above; defines aliases</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td><code>DISTINCT</code></td><td>Remove duplicate output rows</td><td>output columns</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td><code>ORDER BY</code></td><td>Sort</td><td>output aliases are allowed here</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td><code>LIMIT</code></td><td>Keep the first n</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Step through an example below (click a clause):</p>\n"},{"k":"w","src":"a","name":"execorder","props":{}},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-three-consequences-you-will-hit-constantly\">Three consequences you will hit constantly</h3>\n<p><strong>1. Aliases are invisible in <code>WHERE</code>.</strong> <code>WHERE</code> runs before <code>SELECT</code> defines the alias.</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> name, salary <span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">12</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> annual <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> employees <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> annual <span class=\"hljs-operator\">&gt;</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">900000</span>;\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\">-- PostgreSQL: ERROR: column &quot;annual&quot; does not exist</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\">-- (MySQL and SQLite are more lenient, which is why you must not rely on it)</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> name, salary <span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">12</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> annual <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> employees <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> salary <span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">12</span> <span class=\"hljs-operator\">&gt;</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">900000</span>;   <span class=\"hljs-comment\">-- repeat the expression</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\">-- or wrap it:</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> name, salary <span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">12</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> annual <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> employees) t <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> annual <span class=\"hljs-operator\">&gt;</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">900000</span>;</code></pre></div>\n<p><strong>2. Window functions cannot be used in <code>WHERE</code>.</strong> They are computed in step 5, after <code>WHERE</code> has already run. Put the window in a CTE or subquery and filter outside:</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-comment\">-- WRONG:  SELECT * FROM employees WHERE ROW_NUMBER() OVER (...) = 1;</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WITH</span> ranked <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> (\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> e.<span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span>, <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">ROW_NUMBER</span>() <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> dept_id <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> salary <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">DESC</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> rn\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> employees e\n)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> ranked <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> rn <span class=\"hljs-operator\">=</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>;          <span class=\"hljs-comment\">-- the highest-paid person in each department</span></code></pre></div>\n<p>(Some engines such as Snowflake and BigQuery offer <code>QUALIFY rn = 1</code> as a shortcut, but the CTE pattern works everywhere.)</p>\n<p><strong>3. <code>WHERE</code> vs <code>HAVING</code>.</strong> Filter rows with <code>WHERE</code> (cheaper: fewer rows reach the grouping), filter aggregates with <code>HAVING</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> dept_id, <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">COUNT</span>(<span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> headcount\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> employees\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> hire_date <span class=\"hljs-operator\">&gt;=</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2020-01-01&#x27;</span>     <span class=\"hljs-comment\">-- before grouping</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> dept_id\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">HAVING</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">COUNT</span>(<span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span>) <span class=\"hljs-operator\">&gt;=</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>;               <span class=\"hljs-comment\">-- after grouping</span></code></pre></div>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<aside class=\"callout tip\"><div class=\"callout-t\">A fast mental check</div><div class=\"callout-b\"><p>When an error says a name is unknown, ask: &quot;Has the step that creates this name already run?&quot; If not, repeat the expression, use a subquery or CTE, or move the filter.</p>\n</div></aside>"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m2-execution-order~1","q":"Why is <code>ORDER BY annual</code> allowed when <code>WHERE annual &gt; ...</code> is not?","a":"<p>Logical order: <code>FROM</code>, <code>WHERE</code>, <code>GROUP BY</code>, <code>HAVING</code>, <code>SELECT</code> (aliases and window functions created), <code>DISTINCT</code>, <code>ORDER BY</code>, <code>LIMIT</code>. The alias is defined in the <code>SELECT</code> step, so any clause that runs after it (<code>ORDER BY</code>, <code>LIMIT</code>) may use it; clauses before it (<code>WHERE</code>, <code>GROUP BY</code>, <code>HAVING</code> in most engines) may not. This is a logical order: the optimiser may physically reorder work as long as the result is identical.</p>\n","s":"<code>ORDER BY</code> runs after <code>SELECT</code>, when the alias already exists; <code>WHERE</code> runs before."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m2-execution-order~ca0_1","q":"How do you keep only the highest-paid person per department using ROW_NUMBER?","a":"<p>Compute <code>ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY dept ORDER BY salary DESC)</code> in a CTE, then <code>WHERE rn = 1</code> outside, since windows cannot be filtered in WHERE.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m2-execution-order~ca0_2","q":"Why is <code>ORDER BY annual</code> allowed but <code>WHERE annual &gt; 9</code> not?","a":"<p>ORDER BY runs after SELECT created the alias; WHERE runs before.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m2-execution-order~ca0_3","q":"When should a filter go in WHERE rather than HAVING?","a":"<p>Whenever it concerns raw rows, not aggregates: it shrinks the data before grouping.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m2-execution-order~ta1_1","q":"<p>Why does this query raise an error, and how do you fix it?</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> user_id, <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">SUM</span>(amount) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> spend\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> orders\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> spend <span class=\"hljs-operator\">&gt;</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">2000</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> user_id;</code></pre></div>\n","a":"<p>WHERE runs before GROUP BY and before SELECT, so neither the sum nor the alias <code>spend</code> exists yet. A condition on an aggregate belongs in HAVING, which runs after grouping: <code>... GROUP BY user_id HAVING SUM(amount) &gt; 2000</code>. (Some databases accept the alias in HAVING; repeating <code>SUM(amount)</code> works everywhere.)</p>\n"}]},"m2-ranking":{"id":"m2-ranking","slug":"row-number-vs-rank-vs-dense-rank","m":2,"title":"ROW_NUMBER vs RANK vs DENSE_RANK","min":15,"type":"learn","priority":"Highest","prereq":["m2-execution-order"],"tags":["sql","window-functions"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"The anatomy of a window function and the difference between ROW_NUMBER, RANK and DENSE_RANK, plus LAG/LEAD, running totals and frame clauses.","keyTerms":["ROW_NUMBER","RANK","DENSE_RANK","NTILE","LAG","LEAD","PARTITION BY","frame","ROWS vs RANGE"],"interviewQs":["What is the difference between RANK, DENSE_RANK and ROW_NUMBER?","What does PARTITION BY do, and how is it different from GROUP BY?","Why does LAST_VALUE sometimes return the current row's value?"],"mistakes":["Using RANK when you need exactly N rows per group","Forgetting ORDER BY inside OVER","Relying on ROW_NUMBER order for ties without a tie-breaker"],"practice":"Rank the practice employees by salary three ways and explain the differences on the tied rows.","related":["m2-execution-order","m2-level-2","m2-level-3"],"points":"Value - ROW_NUMBER - RANK - DENSE_RANK\n100 - 1 - 1 - 1\n100 - 2 - 1 - 1\n90 - 3 - 3 - 2\n80 - 4 - 4 - 3"},"intuitive":"<p>Imagine a race where two runners tie for second place. &quot;Position in the list&quot; (ROW_NUMBER) still gives everyone a different number: 1, 2, 3, 4. &quot;Olympic ranking&quot; (RANK) gives both silver medals a 2 and skips bronze, so the next runner is 4. &quot;Dense ranking&quot; (DENSE_RANK) gives both a 2 and the next runner gets 3, no skipping. A window function is just &quot;look at my neighbours in this ordered list and tell me something&quot;, without squashing the list into one row.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Top-N per group, running totals and &quot;compare to the previous row&quot; are the most-asked SQL patterns, and they all use windows.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> ROW_NUMBER never ties, RANK skips after ties, DENSE_RANK does not.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;A window function computes a value for each row using nearby rows without collapsing them. The syntax is function OVER with optional PARTITION BY for groups, ORDER BY, and a frame. For ranking: ROW_NUMBER gives unique numbers even for ties, RANK gives ties the same number and leaves gaps after, DENSE_RANK gives ties the same number with no gaps. So with salaries 100, 100, 90 the ranks are 1, 1, 3 and dense ranks 1, 1, 2. LAG and LEAD look at the previous or next row, and SUM OVER with ORDER BY makes a running total. The catch: decide how ties behave, and note that LAST_VALUE needs an explicit frame because the default ends at the current row.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Three ranking functions on one department","html":"<p>Sales salaries in the practice data: Asha 90,000; Ravi 70,000; Meera 70,000; Karan 50,000. Rank them from highest to lowest within the department.</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> name, salary,\n  <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">ROW_NUMBER</span>() <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> dept_id <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> salary <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">DESC</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> rn,\n  <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">RANK</span>()       <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> dept_id <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> salary <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">DESC</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> rnk,\n  <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">DENSE_RANK</span>() <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> dept_id <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> salary <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">DESC</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> drnk\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> employees <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> dept_id <span class=\"hljs-operator\">=</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">10</span>;</code></pre></div>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>PARTITION BY dept_id</strong> says &quot;rank inside each department separately&quot;. <strong>ORDER BY salary DESC</strong> says &quot;highest first&quot;.</li>\n<li><strong>Line them up:</strong> 90k, 70k, 70k, 50k.</li>\n<li><strong>Fill the table.</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>name</th><th>salary</th><th>ROW_NUMBER</th><th>RANK</th><th>DENSE_RANK</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Asha</td><td>90,000</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Ravi</td><td>70,000</td><td>2</td><td>2</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>Meera</td><td>70,000</td><td>3</td><td>2</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>Karan</td><td>50,000</td><td>4</td><td><strong>4</strong></td><td><strong>3</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ol start=\"4\">\n<li><strong>Read the difference at the tie.</strong> ROW_NUMBER gives Ravi and Meera different numbers; which one gets 2 is arbitrary. RANK gives both 2 and then <em>skips</em> 3. DENSE_RANK gives both 2 and continues with 3.</li>\n<li><strong>Choose by the question.</strong> &quot;Exactly one row per person in order&quot;: ROW_NUMBER (add a tie-breaker such as <code>emp_id</code>). &quot;The second-highest salary&quot;: DENSE_RANK = 2, which gives 70,000. &quot;Competition-style positions&quot;: RANK.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> The three functions differ only at ties, so the first question to ask about any ranking problem is &quot;what should happen to ties?&quot;</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-anatomy","text":"Anatomy","level":3},{"id":"h-ranking-on-real-ties","text":"Ranking on real ties","level":3},{"id":"h-looking-at-neighbours-lag-and-lead","text":"Looking at neighbours: LAG and LEAD","level":3},{"id":"h-aggregates-as-windows-and-the-frame","text":"Aggregates as windows, and the frame","level":3},{"id":"h-percent-of-group-without-a-join","text":"Percent of group, without a join","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Value</th><th>ROW_NUMBER</th><th>RANK</th><th>DENSE_RANK</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>100</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>100</td><td>2</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>90</td><td>3</td><td>3</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>80</td><td>4</td><td>4</td><td>3</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-anatomy\">Anatomy</h3>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">function</span>(args) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">OVER</span> (\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> col_a        <span class=\"hljs-comment\">-- restart the calculation for each group (optional)</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> col_b            <span class=\"hljs-comment\">-- the order inside the window (needed for ranking, LAG, running totals)</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ROWS</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BETWEEN</span> ... <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AND</span> ...  <span class=\"hljs-comment\">-- the frame: which neighbouring rows are included (optional)</span>\n)</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>PARTITION BY</code> splits rows into independent windows like <code>GROUP BY</code> does, but <strong>keeps every row</strong>. Without <code>PARTITION BY</code> the whole result is one window.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-ranking-on-real-ties\">Ranking on real ties</h3>\n<p>Department 10 of the practice data has Ravi and Meera tied at 70,000:</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> name, salary,\n       <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">ROW_NUMBER</span>() <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> salary <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">DESC</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> rn,\n       <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">RANK</span>()       <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> salary <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">DESC</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> rk,\n       <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">DENSE_RANK</span>() <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> salary <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">DESC</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> dr\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> employees <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> dept_id <span class=\"hljs-operator\">=</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">10</span>;</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>name</th><th>salary</th><th>rn</th><th>rk</th><th>dr</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Asha</td><td>90000</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Ravi</td><td>70000</td><td>2</td><td>2</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>Meera</td><td>70000</td><td>3</td><td>2</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>Karan</td><td>50000</td><td>4</td><td>4</td><td>3</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>(<code>ROW_NUMBER</code> assigns 2 and 3 to the tied rows in an arbitrary order unless you add a tie-breaker like <code>ORDER BY salary DESC, emp_id</code>.)</p>\n<p>Try your own values here:</p>\n"},{"k":"w","src":"a","name":"windowfn","props":{}},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<p><strong>Which one to pick.</strong> &quot;The top 3 people per department, including everyone tied&quot;: <code>DENSE_RANK &lt;= 3</code> (or <code>RANK &lt;= 3</code>). &quot;Exactly one row per group&quot; (latest order per user, de-duplication): <code>ROW_NUMBER = 1</code> with a deterministic tie-breaker. &quot;Nth highest value&quot;: <code>DENSE_RANK = N</code> so ties do not hide lower values.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-looking-at-neighbours-lag-and-lead\">Looking at neighbours: LAG and LEAD</h3>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> name, salary,\n       <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">LAG</span>(salary)  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> salary) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> prev_sal,\n       <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">LEAD</span>(salary) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> salary) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> next_sal\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> employees <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> dept_id <span class=\"hljs-operator\">=</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">20</span>;</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>name</th><th>salary</th><th>prev_sal</th><th>next_sal</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Dev</td><td>60000</td><td>NULL</td><td>60000</td></tr><tr><td>Nina</td><td>60000</td><td>60000</td><td>80000</td></tr><tr><td>Isha</td><td>80000</td><td>60000</td><td>NULL</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><code>LAG(x, n, default)</code> looks <code>n</code> rows back (default 1) and returns <code>default</code> instead of NULL at the edge. Use it for month-over-month change, days between orders and session boundaries.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-aggregates-as-windows-and-the-frame\">Aggregates as windows, and the frame</h3>\n<p><code>SUM(...) OVER (ORDER BY ...)</code> is a running total. The <strong>frame</strong> decides which rows are included for each row:</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-built_in\">SUM</span>(salary) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> salary <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ROWS</span>  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BETWEEN</span> UNBOUNDED PRECEDING <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AND</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">CURRENT</span> <span class=\"hljs-type\">ROW</span>)   <span class=\"hljs-comment\">-- by position</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">SUM</span>(salary) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> salary <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">RANGE</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BETWEEN</span> UNBOUNDED PRECEDING <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AND</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">CURRENT</span> <span class=\"hljs-type\">ROW</span>)   <span class=\"hljs-comment\">-- by value (ties included)</span></code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>name</th><th>salary</th><th>ROWS running sum</th><th>RANGE running sum</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Karan</td><td>50000</td><td>50000</td><td>50000</td></tr><tr><td>Meera</td><td>70000</td><td>190000</td><td>190000</td></tr><tr><td>Ravi</td><td>70000</td><td>120000</td><td>190000</td></tr><tr><td>Asha</td><td>90000</td><td>280000</td><td>280000</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><code>ROWS</code> counts physical rows; <code>RANGE</code> treats rows with equal ORDER BY values as one &quot;peer group&quot; and includes all of them. When you write <code>ORDER BY</code> without a frame, the default is <code>RANGE BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW</code>, which surprises people with ties.</p>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<aside class=\"callout trap\"><div class=\"callout-t\">LAST_VALUE needs an explicit frame</div><div class=\"callout-b\"><p>With the default frame, the window ends at the current row, so <code>LAST_VALUE(salary) OVER (ORDER BY salary)</code> just returns the current row&#39;s salary. Write <code>ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND UNBOUNDED FOLLOWING</code> to get the true last value of the partition (here 90,000 for everyone).</p>\n</div></aside>"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-percent-of-group-without-a-join\">Percent of group, without a join</h3>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> name, dept_id, salary,\n       ROUND(<span class=\"hljs-number\">100.0</span> <span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span> salary <span class=\"hljs-operator\">/</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">SUM</span>(salary) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> dept_id), <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> pct_of_dept\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> employees;</code></pre></div>\n<p>Asha earns 32.1% of Sales&#39; 280,000 payroll; Zoya earns 63.3% of Ops&#39;. The window gives the department total on every row, which a plain <code>GROUP BY</code> cannot do without a self-join.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m2-ranking~1","q":"Write a query that returns the second-highest salary in each department, correctly handling ties for first place.","a":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WITH</span> r <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> (\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> dept_id, name, salary,\n         <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">DENSE_RANK</span>() <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> dept_id <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> salary <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">DESC</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> rk\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> employees\n)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> dept_id, name, salary <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> r <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> rk <span class=\"hljs-operator\">=</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>;</code></pre></div>\n<p>If two people tie for the top salary, <code>DENSE_RANK</code> still gives the next distinct salary rank 2; <code>RANK</code> would skip to 3 and <code>ROW_NUMBER</code> would wrongly treat the second tied person as &quot;second&quot;. Decide and state the tie semantics the interviewer wants.</p>\n","s":"<code>DENSE_RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY dept_id ORDER BY salary DESC)</code> and filter for 2 in an outer query."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m2-ranking~ca0_1","q":"Salaries 90, 70, 70, 50. Give ROW_NUMBER, RANK, DENSE_RANK for 50.","a":"<p>4, 4 and 3.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m2-ranking~ca0_2","q":"Which function gives &quot;the Nth highest salary&quot; robustly, and why?","a":"<p>DENSE_RANK = N: ties do not hide lower values, unlike RANK (gaps) or ROW_NUMBER (arbitrary).</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m2-ranking~ca0_3","q":"How does PARTITION BY differ from GROUP BY?","a":"<p>Both split into groups, but PARTITION BY keeps every row; GROUP BY collapses each group to one row.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m2-ranking~ta1_1","q":"<p>The Data department has Isha 80,000, Dev 60,000 and Nina 60,000. Give ROW_NUMBER, RANK and DENSE_RANK for each, and say which rows <code>WHERE DENSE_RANK = 2</code> returns.</p>\n","a":"<p>Isha: 1, 1, 1. Dev and Nina: ROW_NUMBER 2 and 3 (in an arbitrary order), RANK 2 and 2, DENSE_RANK 2 and 2. <code>DENSE_RANK = 2</code> returns both Dev and Nina: the &quot;second-highest salary&quot; is 60,000, earned by two people. With ROW_NUMBER = 2 you would get just one of them, chosen arbitrarily.</p>\n"}]},"m2-schema":{"id":"m2-schema","slug":"practice-schema-used-in-every-problem","m":2,"title":"Practice schema used in every problem","min":5,"type":"learn","priority":"Highest","prereq":[],"tags":["sql","practice","setup"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"The tables used in every SQL problem, a script to create them with small sample data, and a cheat sheet of dialect differences.","keyTerms":["practice schema","sample data","SQLite","PostgreSQL","dialect differences"],"interviewQs":["How would you model users, orders and order items?","What grain does each table have?"],"mistakes":["Not checking the grain of a table before joining","Copying a Postgres query into MySQL or SQLite without checking functions"],"practice":"Load the sample data into any SQL engine and run the first three problems before opening the solutions.","related":["m2-level-1","m2-checklist"],"points":""},"intuitive":"<p>Before you can practise cooking you need a kitchen with some food in it. This page gives you a small, pretend company: employees and departments, customers who place orders, the items inside each order, the products, and a log of who logged in or clicked on what day. The data is tiny on purpose so you can work out every answer by hand, then check it by running the query.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Interview SQL is always &quot;given these tables...&quot;, so knowing what each row means (its grain) is half the answer.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Always ask &quot;one row equals what?&quot; before joining two tables.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;Before joining tables I ask what one row means, the grain. Orders has one row per order, order items one per product line, so joining them multiplies order rows, which inflates any sum of order amounts. On the sample data, summing order amounts after the join gives 28,800 instead of the true 21,500. So I either aggregate before joining or sum only item-level columns. I also check keys, NULLs and duplicates, like repeated login rows. The catch: wrong grain gives plausible but wrong numbers, and it is the most common SQL mistake in interviews.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"See fan-out with your own eyes","html":"<p>Orders 1 and 3 together are worth 1,500 + 2,500 = <strong>4,000</strong>. Order 3 contains two products. Watch what a join does.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>The grain of each table.</strong> <code>orders</code>: one row per order. <code>order_items</code>: one row per product inside an order.</li>\n<li><strong>Join them.</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>order_id</th><th>orders.amount</th><th>product_id</th><th>line value (qty × price)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>1,500</td><td>1</td><td>1,500</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>2,500</td><td>2</td><td>1,000</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>2,500</td><td>1</td><td>1,500</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ol start=\"3\">\n<li><strong>Sum the order amount after the join:</strong> 1,500 + 2,500 + 2,500 = <strong>6,500</strong>. Order 3 was counted twice because it has two item rows.</li>\n<li><strong>Sum the line values instead:</strong> 1,500 + 1,000 + 1,500 = <strong>4,000</strong>. Correct, because each line is counted once at its own grain.</li>\n<li><strong>Across the whole dataset</strong> the wrong sum is 28,800 against a true total of 21,500.</li>\n<li><strong>The habit.</strong> Before every join, say &quot;one row of A is ..., one row of B is ...&quot;. If B has many rows per A row, either aggregate B first or sum only B&#39;s columns.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Fan-out produces believable numbers that are simply too big. It is invisible unless you check the grain and the row counts.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-the-tables-and-their-grain","text":"The tables and their grain","level":3},{"id":"h-load-the-sample-data","text":"Load the sample data","level":3},{"id":"h-dialect-cheat-sheet","text":"Dialect cheat sheet","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">employees(emp_id, name, dept_id, salary, manager_id, hire_date)\ndepartments(dept_id, dept_name)\nusers(user_id, signup_date, city, device)\norders(order_id, user_id, order_date, amount, status)\norder_items(order_id, product_id, qty, price)\nproducts(product_id, category, product_name)\nlogins(user_id, login_date)\nevents(user_id, event_time, event_name)    <span class=\"hljs-comment\">-- e.g. &#x27;view&#x27;,&#x27;cart&#x27;,&#x27;purchase&#x27;</span></code></pre></div>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-the-tables-and-their-grain\">The tables and their grain</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Table</th><th>One row is...</th><th>Key columns</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>employees</code></td><td>one employee</td><td><code>emp_id</code>, <code>dept_id</code>, <code>manager_id</code> (self-reference), <code>salary</code>, <code>hire_date</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>departments</code></td><td>one department</td><td><code>dept_id</code>, <code>dept_name</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>users</code></td><td>one registered user</td><td><code>user_id</code>, <code>signup_date</code>, <code>city</code>, <code>device</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>orders</code></td><td>one order</td><td><code>order_id</code>, <code>user_id</code>, <code>order_date</code>, <code>amount</code>, <code>status</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>order_items</code></td><td>one product line within an order</td><td><code>order_id</code>, <code>product_id</code>, <code>qty</code>, <code>price</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>products</code></td><td>one product</td><td><code>product_id</code>, <code>category</code>, <code>product_name</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>logins</code></td><td>one login event (duplicates possible)</td><td><code>user_id</code>, <code>login_date</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>events</code></td><td>one click-stream event</td><td><code>user_id</code>, <code>event_time</code>, <code>event_name</code> (<code>view</code>, <code>cart</code>, <code>purchase</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><code>customers</code></td><td>one customer row (used for the duplicate-email problems)</td><td><code>id</code>, <code>email</code>, <code>name</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>An order has <strong>many</strong> <code>order_items</code>, so joining <code>orders</code> to <code>order_items</code> multiplies order rows (fan-out), which is why summing <code>orders.amount</code> after that join overcounts. Keep the grain in mind.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-load-the-sample-data\">Load the sample data</h3>\n<p>The script runs unchanged on SQLite (3.25 or newer), PostgreSQL and, with minor type changes, MySQL 8. Save it as <code>sample.sql</code> and run <code>sqlite3 practice.db &lt; sample.sql</code>, or paste it into any SQL playground.</p>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<details class=\"more\"><summary>Click to show the full setup script</summary><div class=\"more-b\"><div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">CREATE TABLE</span> departments (dept_id <span class=\"hljs-type\">INT</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">PRIMARY KEY</span>, dept_name TEXT);\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">INSERT INTO</span> departments <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">VALUES</span> (<span class=\"hljs-number\">10</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Sales&#x27;</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">20</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Data&#x27;</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">30</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Ops&#x27;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">CREATE TABLE</span> employees (emp_id <span class=\"hljs-type\">INT</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">PRIMARY KEY</span>, name TEXT, dept_id <span class=\"hljs-type\">INT</span>, salary <span class=\"hljs-type\">INT</span>, manager_id <span class=\"hljs-type\">INT</span>, hire_date <span class=\"hljs-type\">DATE</span>);\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">INSERT INTO</span> employees <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">VALUES</span>\n (<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Asha&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">10</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">90000</span>,<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">NULL</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2019-04-01&#x27;</span>), (<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Ravi&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">10</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">70000</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2020-06-15&#x27;</span>),\n (<span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Meera&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">10</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">70000</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2021-01-10&#x27;</span>),   (<span class=\"hljs-number\">4</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Karan&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">10</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">50000</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2022-09-01&#x27;</span>),\n (<span class=\"hljs-number\">5</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Isha&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">20</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">80000</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2020-02-20&#x27;</span>),    (<span class=\"hljs-number\">6</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Dev&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">20</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">60000</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">5</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2021-07-05&#x27;</span>),\n (<span class=\"hljs-number\">7</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Nina&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">20</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">60000</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">5</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2023-03-12&#x27;</span>),    (<span class=\"hljs-number\">8</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Omar&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">30</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">55000</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2021-11-30&#x27;</span>),\n (<span class=\"hljs-number\">9</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Zoya&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">30</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">95000</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">8</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2022-05-18&#x27;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">CREATE TABLE</span> customers (id <span class=\"hljs-type\">INT</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">PRIMARY KEY</span>, email TEXT, name TEXT);\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">INSERT INTO</span> customers <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">VALUES</span> (<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;a@x.com&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Anu&#x27;</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;b@x.com&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Bala&#x27;</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;a@x.com&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Anu K&#x27;</span>),\n (<span class=\"hljs-number\">4</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;c@x.com&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Chitra&#x27;</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">5</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;b@x.com&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Bala S&#x27;</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">6</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;d@x.com&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Deepa&#x27;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">CREATE TABLE</span> users (user_id <span class=\"hljs-type\">INT</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">PRIMARY KEY</span>, signup_date <span class=\"hljs-type\">DATE</span>, city TEXT, device TEXT);\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">INSERT INTO</span> users <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">VALUES</span>\n (<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-01-02&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Bengaluru&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;android&#x27;</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-01-03&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Mumbai&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;ios&#x27;</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-01-03&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Delhi&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;android&#x27;</span>),\n (<span class=\"hljs-number\">4</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-01-10&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Bengaluru&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;web&#x27;</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">5</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-02-01&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Pune&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;ios&#x27;</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">6</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-02-02&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Delhi&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;android&#x27;</span>),\n (<span class=\"hljs-number\">7</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-02-15&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Mumbai&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;web&#x27;</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">8</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-03-01&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Bengaluru&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;android&#x27;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">CREATE TABLE</span> products (product_id <span class=\"hljs-type\">INT</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">PRIMARY KEY</span>, category TEXT, product_name TEXT);\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">INSERT INTO</span> products <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">VALUES</span> (<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Electronics&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Earbuds&#x27;</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Electronics&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Power bank&#x27;</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Fashion&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;T-shirt&#x27;</span>),\n (<span class=\"hljs-number\">4</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Fashion&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Sneakers&#x27;</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">5</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Grocery&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Basmati rice&#x27;</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">6</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Grocery&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Tea&#x27;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">CREATE TABLE</span> orders (order_id <span class=\"hljs-type\">INT</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">PRIMARY KEY</span>, user_id <span class=\"hljs-type\">INT</span>, order_date <span class=\"hljs-type\">DATE</span>, amount <span class=\"hljs-type\">INT</span>, status TEXT);\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">INSERT INTO</span> orders <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">VALUES</span>\n (<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-01-05&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">1500</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;delivered&#x27;</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-01-20&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">800</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;delivered&#x27;</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-01-06&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">2500</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;delivered&#x27;</span>),\n (<span class=\"hljs-number\">4</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-01-15&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">400</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;cancelled&#x27;</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">5</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-02-03&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">1200</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;delivered&#x27;</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">6</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">4</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-01-12&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">3000</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;delivered&#x27;</span>),\n (<span class=\"hljs-number\">7</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-02-10&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">600</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;delivered&#x27;</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">8</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-02-14&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">900</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;delivered&#x27;</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">9</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">5</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-02-05&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">1800</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;delivered&#x27;</span>),\n (<span class=\"hljs-number\">10</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">6</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-02-20&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">700</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;delivered&#x27;</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">11</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-03-02&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">2200</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;delivered&#x27;</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">12</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-03-05&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">300</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;delivered&#x27;</span>),\n (<span class=\"hljs-number\">13</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">5</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-03-09&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">1100</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;delivered&#x27;</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">14</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">4</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-03-15&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">2600</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;delivered&#x27;</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">15</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">6</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-03-18&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">500</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;delivered&#x27;</span>),\n (<span class=\"hljs-number\">16</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">7</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-03-20&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">1400</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;delivered&#x27;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">CREATE TABLE</span> order_items (order_id <span class=\"hljs-type\">INT</span>, product_id <span class=\"hljs-type\">INT</span>, qty <span class=\"hljs-type\">INT</span>, price <span class=\"hljs-type\">INT</span>);\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">INSERT INTO</span> order_items <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">VALUES</span>\n (<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">1500</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">400</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">1000</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">1500</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">4</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">5</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">400</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">5</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">4</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">1200</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">6</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">1500</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">7</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">6</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">300</span>),\n (<span class=\"hljs-number\">8</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">900</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">9</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">4</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">1800</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">10</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">5</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">700</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">11</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">1500</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">11</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">5</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">700</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">12</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">6</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">300</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">13</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">1100</span>),\n (<span class=\"hljs-number\">14</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">4</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">1800</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">14</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">400</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">15</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">6</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">500</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">16</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">1400</span>);\n\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">CREATE TABLE</span> logins (user_id <span class=\"hljs-type\">INT</span>, login_date <span class=\"hljs-type\">DATE</span>);\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">INSERT INTO</span> logins <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">VALUES</span>\n (<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-01-02&#x27;</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-01-03&#x27;</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-01-03&#x27;</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-01-04&#x27;</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-01-06&#x27;</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-01-07&#x27;</span>),\n (<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-01-03&#x27;</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-01-05&#x27;</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-01-06&#x27;</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-01-07&#x27;</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-01-08&#x27;</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-01-09&#x27;</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-01-10&#x27;</span>),\n (<span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-01-03&#x27;</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-01-04&#x27;</span>),\n (<span class=\"hljs-number\">4</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-01-10&#x27;</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">4</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-01-11&#x27;</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">4</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-01-12&#x27;</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">4</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-01-13&#x27;</span>),\n (<span class=\"hljs-number\">5</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-02-01&#x27;</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">5</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-02-03&#x27;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">CREATE TABLE</span> events (user_id <span class=\"hljs-type\">INT</span>, event_time <span class=\"hljs-type\">TIMESTAMP</span>, event_name TEXT);\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">INSERT INTO</span> events <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">VALUES</span>\n (<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-03-01 10:00:00&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;view&#x27;</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-03-01 10:05:00&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;cart&#x27;</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-03-01 10:20:00&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;purchase&#x27;</span>),\n (<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-03-01 11:00:00&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;view&#x27;</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-03-01 11:10:00&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;cart&#x27;</span>),\n (<span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-03-01 12:00:00&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;view&#x27;</span>),\n (<span class=\"hljs-number\">4</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-03-01 13:00:00&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;view&#x27;</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">4</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-03-01 13:02:00&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;view&#x27;</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">4</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-03-01 13:40:00&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;view&#x27;</span>),\n (<span class=\"hljs-number\">5</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-03-01 14:00:00&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;view&#x27;</span>),(<span class=\"hljs-number\">5</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-03-01 14:30:00&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;purchase&#x27;</span>);</code></pre></div>\n</div></details>"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<p>The data has built-in features so the problems are interesting: user 8 never ordered (anti-join), user 3 has a cancelled order, Ravi and Meera tie on salary (ranking), Zoya earns more than her manager (self-join), user 1 has a login streak plus a duplicate login row (gaps-and-islands, DISTINCT), and user 4 has a 38-minute gap between events (sessionization).</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-dialect-cheat-sheet\">Dialect cheat sheet</h3>\n<p>The course queries are PostgreSQL / standard SQL. When your interview engine differs, translate:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Need</th><th>PostgreSQL</th><th>MySQL 8</th><th>SQLite</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>First day of month</td><td><code>DATE_TRUNC(&#39;month&#39;, d)</code></td><td><code>DATE_FORMAT(d, &#39;%Y-%m-01&#39;)</code></td><td><code>strftime(&#39;%Y-%m-01&#39;, d)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Add days</td><td><code>d + 7</code> or <code>d + INTERVAL &#39;7 days&#39;</code></td><td><code>d + INTERVAL 7 DAY</code></td><td><code>date(d, &#39;+7 days&#39;)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Days between</td><td><code>d2 - d1</code></td><td><code>DATEDIFF(d2, d1)</code></td><td><code>julianday(d2) - julianday(d1)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Cast</td><td><code>x::int</code> or <code>CAST(x AS INT)</code></td><td><code>CAST(x AS SIGNED)</code></td><td><code>CAST(x AS INT)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Median</td><td><code>PERCENTILE_CONT(0.5) WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY x)</code></td><td>window + row numbers</td><td>window + row numbers</td></tr><tr><td>Top N rows</td><td><code>LIMIT n</code></td><td><code>LIMIT n</code></td><td><code>LIMIT n</code></td></tr><tr><td>Conditional count</td><td><code>COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE c)</code></td><td><code>SUM(c)</code> or <code>COUNT(CASE WHEN c THEN 1 END)</code></td><td><code>FILTER</code> (3.30+)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m2-schema~1","q":"Before joining <code>orders</code> to <code>order_items</code>, what must you check, and what goes wrong if you skip it?","a":"<p><code>orders</code> has one row per order; <code>order_items</code> has one row per product line. A join produces one row per <strong>item</strong>, so <code>orders.amount</code> repeats once per item. On the sample data, <code>SUM(o.amount)</code> after the join returns 28,800 instead of the true 21,500. The fix is to aggregate each table to the level you need <em>before</em> joining (or sum only item-level columns such as <code>qty * price</code>).</p>\n","s":"The grain: one order has many items, so order-level columns repeat and sums get inflated."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m2-schema~ca0_1","q":"Why does SUM(orders.amount) double-count after joining order_items?","a":"<p>Each order repeats once per item row (fan-out). Aggregate first or sum qty * price instead.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m2-schema~ca0_2","q":"What is the grain of the <code>logins</code> table and how can it mislead counts?","a":"<p>One row per login event, possibly duplicated per user-day, so COUNT(*) overcounts activity; use DISTINCT user_id, date.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m2-schema~ca0_3","q":"Which sanity check catches a bad join early?","a":"<p>Compare row counts before and after the join and check that totals still reconcile with the source.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m2-schema~ta1_1","q":"<p>Orders 11 (amount 2,200) and 14 (amount 2,600) each have two item rows. If you join <code>orders</code> to <code>order_items</code> for just these two orders, how many rows do you get, and what do <code>SUM(o.amount)</code> and <code>SUM(i.qty * i.price)</code> return?</p>\n","a":"<p>4 rows (two per order). <code>SUM(o.amount)</code> = 2,200 + 2,200 + 2,600 + 2,600 = 9,600, which double-counts. <code>SUM(i.qty * i.price)</code> = (1,500 + 700) + (1,800 + 800) = 4,800, which equals the true total 2,200 + 2,600. The item lines add up to the order amounts, so summing at the item grain is safe.</p>\n"}]},"m2-level-1":{"id":"m2-level-1","slug":"sql-problems-level-1-basics","m":2,"title":"SQL problems, level 1: basics","min":30,"type":"practice","priority":"Highest","prereq":["m2-schema"],"tags":["sql","practice"],"qa":11,"ai":{"scope":"Basic SQL problems: anti-joins, filtering groups, finding and removing duplicates, with verified outputs on the practice data.","keyTerms":["anti-join","HAVING","duplicates","DELETE duplicates","NOT EXISTS","scalar subquery"],"interviewQs":["Find customers who never placed an order (two ways).","Find duplicate emails.","Delete duplicate rows keeping the smallest id."],"mistakes":["NOT IN with NULLs","Deleting with a subquery on the same table in MySQL without a derived table","Filtering after the aggregate with WHERE"],"practice":"Attempt each problem before revealing its answer, then run the solution on the sample data and compare.","related":["m2-checklist","m2-level-2"],"points":"Q: Customers who never placed an order.\nA: Pitfall: NOT IN returns nothing if the subquery contains NULL.\n\nQ: Department-wise average salary, only departments with avg > 50k.\nA: \n\nQ: Find duplicate emails / duplicate rows.\nA: \n\nQ: Delete duplicates keeping the lowest id.\nA: "},"intuitive":"<p>Level 1 is about asking clear questions of one or two tables: &quot;who is missing?&quot;, &quot;which groups are big enough?&quot;, &quot;which rows are copies?&quot;. Finding people who never ordered is like comparing the class list with the list of people who handed in homework: anyone on the first list but not the second is missing. Finding duplicates is grouping identical emails into piles and looking for piles with more than one item.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> These are warm-ups, and clean answers here set the tone for the round.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Missing means LEFT JOIN + IS NULL (or NOT EXISTS); duplicates mean GROUP BY + HAVING COUNT(*) &gt; 1.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;Level one is about missing and duplicate rows. To find customers who never ordered, I LEFT JOIN users to orders and keep rows where the order id is NULL, or use NOT EXISTS, which is safest. To find duplicates, GROUP BY the key and HAVING COUNT star greater than one. To delete duplicates keeping one, keep the minimum id per key, or number rows with ROW_NUMBER partitioned by the key and delete where the number is above one. Group filters go in HAVING, row filters in WHERE. The catch: NOT IN breaks with NULLs, and I test a DELETE as a SELECT first.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Find duplicate emails, then keep one","html":"<p>The <code>customers</code> table has 6 rows. Which emails appear more than once, and how do you remove the extra rows?</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Group by the thing that should be unique.</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> email, <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">COUNT</span>(<span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> n, <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">MIN</span>(id) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> keep_id\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> customers\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> email\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">HAVING</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">COUNT</span>(<span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span>) <span class=\"hljs-operator\">&gt;</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>;</code></pre></div>\n<ol start=\"2\">\n<li><strong>Trace the groups.</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>email</th><th>ids in the group</th><th>n</th><th>keep_id</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"mailto:a@x.com\">a@x.com</a></td><td>1, 3</td><td>2</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"mailto:b@x.com\">b@x.com</a></td><td>2, 5</td><td>2</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"mailto:c@x.com\">c@x.com</a></td><td>4</td><td>1</td><td></td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"mailto:d@x.com\">d@x.com</a></td><td>6</td><td>1</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ol start=\"3\">\n<li><strong>HAVING COUNT(*) &gt; 1</strong> keeps <a href=\"mailto:a@x.com\">a@x.com</a> and <a href=\"mailto:b@x.com\">b@x.com</a>. That answers &quot;which emails are duplicated&quot;.</li>\n<li><strong>Decide which row survives.</strong> A common rule is the lowest id (the first sign-up). The rows to delete are ids 3 and 5.</li>\n<li><strong>Test the delete as a SELECT first.</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> customers c\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> id <span class=\"hljs-operator\">&gt;</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">MIN</span>(id) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> customers c2 <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> c2.email <span class=\"hljs-operator\">=</span> c.email);</code></pre></div>\n<p>   It returns ids 3 and 5. Only then change <code>SELECT *</code> to <code>DELETE</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> &quot;Group by the key, count, filter the groups&quot; finds duplicates. Always preview a DELETE as a SELECT.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-what-each-answer-returns-on-the-sample-data","text":"What each answer returns on the sample data","level":3},{"id":"h-patterns-to-notice","text":"Patterns to notice","level":3},{"id":"h-more-problems-with-solutions","text":"More problems, with solutions","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"qa","id":"2.5#1","src":"c","q":"Customers who never placed an order.","a":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> u.user_id\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> users u\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">LEFT</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">JOIN</span> orders o <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ON</span> o.user_id <span class=\"hljs-operator\">=</span> u.user_id\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> o.order_id <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">IS</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">NULL</span>;\n\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\">-- or</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> user_id <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> users u\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">NOT</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">EXISTS</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> orders o <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> o.user_id <span class=\"hljs-operator\">=</span> u.user_id);</code></pre></div>\n<p>Pitfall: <code>NOT IN</code> returns nothing if the subquery contains NULL.</p>\n","s":"<code>LEFT JOIN orders ... WHERE o.order_id IS NULL</code>, or <code>NOT EXISTS</code>; avoid <code>NOT IN</code> when NULLs are possible.","label":"P1"},{"k":"qa","id":"2.5#2","src":"c","q":"Department-wise average salary, only departments with avg &gt; 50k.","a":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> d.dept_name, <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">AVG</span>(e.salary) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> avg_sal\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> employees e <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">JOIN</span> departments d <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">USING</span> (dept_id)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> d.dept_name\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">HAVING</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">AVG</span>(e.salary) <span class=\"hljs-operator\">&gt;</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">50000</span>;</code></pre></div>\n","s":"Join, <code>GROUP BY dept_name</code>, then <code>HAVING AVG(salary) &gt; 50000</code>.","label":"P2"},{"k":"qa","id":"2.5#3","src":"c","q":"Find duplicate emails / duplicate rows.","a":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> email, <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">COUNT</span>(<span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> customers <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> email <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">HAVING</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">COUNT</span>(<span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span>) <span class=\"hljs-operator\">&gt;</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>;</code></pre></div>\n","s":"<code>GROUP BY email HAVING COUNT(*) &gt; 1</code>.","label":"P3"},{"k":"qa","id":"2.5#4","src":"c","q":"Delete duplicates keeping the lowest id.","a":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">DELETE</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> customers\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> id <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">NOT</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">IN</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">MIN</span>(id) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> customers <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> email);\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\">-- MySQL requires wrapping the subquery in a derived table.</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\">-- Window version (Postgres): </span>\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">DELETE</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> customers <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> id <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">IN</span> (\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> id <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> (\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> id, <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">ROW_NUMBER</span>() <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> email <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> id) rn <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> customers\n  ) t <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> rn <span class=\"hljs-operator\">&gt;</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>);</code></pre></div>\n","s":"Keep <code>MIN(id)</code> per email and delete the rest, or delete rows where <code>ROW_NUMBER() ... &gt; 1</code>.","label":"P4"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-what-each-answer-returns-on-the-sample-data\">What each answer returns on the sample data</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>P1 never ordered</td><td>user 8</td></tr><tr><td>P2 departments with average salary above 65,000 (threshold lowered to suit the tiny data)</td><td>Data 66,667 · Ops 75,000 · Sales 70,000</td></tr><tr><td>P3 duplicate emails</td><td><code>a@x.com</code> (2) and <code>b@x.com</code> (2)</td></tr><tr><td>P4 after deleting duplicates</td><td>customers 1, 2, 4, 6 remain</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"h-patterns-to-notice\">Patterns to notice</h3>\n<p><strong>Anti-join (P1).</strong> Three equivalent forms: <code>LEFT JOIN ... WHERE right.key IS NULL</code>, <code>NOT EXISTS</code>, and <code>NOT IN</code> (dangerous with NULLs). <code>NOT EXISTS</code> is the safest and often the fastest.</p>\n<p><strong>Aggregate filters (P2).</strong> <code>HAVING AVG(...) &gt; x</code>. If you first need to restrict rows (only delivered orders, only 2024), that restriction belongs in <code>WHERE</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Duplicates (P3, P4).</strong> To <em>find</em> them: <code>GROUP BY</code> the defining column(s) with <code>HAVING COUNT(*) &gt; 1</code>. To <em>delete</em> them keeping one row per group: either keep <code>MIN(id)</code> per group, or number rows with <code>ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY email ORDER BY id)</code> and delete where <code>rn &gt; 1</code>.</p>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<aside class=\"callout tip\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Test a DELETE safely</div><div class=\"callout-b\"><p>Turn it into a SELECT first (<code>SELECT * FROM customers WHERE id NOT IN (...)</code>) and read the rows. Then wrap the real DELETE in a transaction (<code>BEGIN; ... ROLLBACK;</code>) until you are sure.</p>\n</div></aside>"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-more-problems-with-solutions\">More problems, with solutions</h3>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m2-level-1~1","q":"Users and order count and total spend for delivered orders, only for users with at least 2 delivered orders.","a":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> user_id, <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">COUNT</span>(<span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> n_orders, <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">SUM</span>(amount) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> total, ROUND(<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">AVG</span>(amount), <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> avg_amt\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> orders\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> status <span class=\"hljs-operator\">=</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;delivered&#x27;</span>            <span class=\"hljs-comment\">-- row filter first</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> user_id\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">HAVING</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">COUNT</span>(<span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span>) <span class=\"hljs-operator\">&gt;=</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>;                 <span class=\"hljs-comment\">-- then the group filter</span></code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>user_id</th><th>n_orders</th><th>total</th><th>avg_amt</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>4</td><td>5100</td><td>1275.0</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>3</td><td>3700</td><td>1233.3</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>2</td><td>5600</td><td>2800.0</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>2</td><td>2900</td><td>1450.0</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>2</td><td>1200</td><td>600.0</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>User 3 is excluded because one of their two orders was cancelled.</p>\n","s":"Filter rows with <code>WHERE status = &#39;delivered&#39;</code>, then <code>GROUP BY user_id HAVING COUNT(*) &gt;= 2</code>."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m2-level-1~2","q":"List users who signed up in January 2025.","a":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> user_id, signup_date\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> users\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> signup_date <span class=\"hljs-operator\">&gt;=</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-01-01&#x27;</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AND</span> signup_date <span class=\"hljs-operator\">&lt;</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-02-01&#x27;</span>;</code></pre></div>\n<p>Returns users 1, 2, 3 and 4. Prefer <code>&gt;= start AND &lt; next_start</code> over <code>BETWEEN</code>: it is correct for timestamps too (<code>BETWEEN &#39;2025-01-01&#39; AND &#39;2025-01-31&#39;</code> would miss anything on the 31st after midnight).</p>\n","s":"Use a half-open range: <code>&gt;= &#39;2025-01-01&#39; AND &lt; &#39;2025-02-01&#39;</code>."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m2-level-1~3","q":"Which orders are above the overall average order amount?","a":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> order_id, amount <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> orders\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> amount <span class=\"hljs-operator\">&gt;</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">AVG</span>(amount) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> orders)   <span class=\"hljs-comment\">-- average = 1,343.75</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> amount <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">DESC</span>;</code></pre></div>\n<p>Orders 6, 14, 3, 11, 9, 1 and 16 qualify. The subquery runs once and returns a single value.</p>\n","s":"A scalar subquery: <code>WHERE amount &gt; (SELECT AVG(amount) FROM orders)</code>."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m2-level-1~ca0_1","q":"Users who signed up in January 2025: write the date filter safely.","a":"<p><code>signup_date &gt;= &#39;2025-01-01&#39; AND signup_date &lt; &#39;2025-02-01&#39;</code>: a half-open range that works for timestamps too.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m2-level-1~ca0_2","q":"How do you list emails that appear more than once?","a":"<p><code>SELECT email, COUNT(*) FROM customers GROUP BY email HAVING COUNT(*) &gt; 1</code>.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m2-level-1~ca0_3","q":"Users with 2 or more delivered orders: where does each condition go?","a":"<p><code>status = &#39;delivered&#39;</code> in WHERE (rows), <code>COUNT(*) &gt;= 2</code> in HAVING (groups).</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m2-level-1~ta1_1","q":"<p>Write a query for &quot;users who have never placed an order&quot; on the practice data, and say which user it returns.</p>\n","a":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> u.user_id\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> users u\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">NOT</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">EXISTS</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> orders o <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> o.user_id <span class=\"hljs-operator\">=</span> u.user_id);</code></pre></div>\n<p>It returns user 8, the only user with no row in <code>orders</code>. The equivalent anti-join is <code>LEFT JOIN orders o ON o.user_id = u.user_id WHERE o.order_id IS NULL</code>. Avoid <code>NOT IN (SELECT user_id FROM orders)</code>: if any <code>user_id</code> in orders were NULL, it would return nothing.</p>\n"}]},"m2-level-2":{"id":"m2-level-2","slug":"sql-problems-level-2-ranking-and-top-n","m":2,"title":"SQL problems, level 2: ranking and top-N","min":30,"type":"practice","priority":"Highest","prereq":["m2-ranking","m2-level-1"],"tags":["sql","practice","window-functions"],"qa":11,"ai":{"scope":"Ranking and top-N problems: Nth highest, top 3 per department, self joins and top category per month, with tie handling and verified outputs.","keyTerms":["DENSE_RANK","top-N per group","self join","CTE","ROW_NUMBER","tie handling"],"interviewQs":["Find the Nth highest salary.","Top 3 earners in each department. How do ties change your answer?","Employees earning more than their manager."],"mistakes":["Using LIMIT/OFFSET for Nth highest with duplicates","Using ROW_NUMBER when ties should be included","Filtering on the window function in WHERE"],"practice":"Solve P5 to P8 without looking, then change the tie rule (RANK instead of DENSE_RANK) and predict how the output changes.","related":["m2-ranking","m2-execution-order","m2-level-3"],"points":"Q: Nth highest salary (overall).\nA: \n\nQ: Top 3 earners in each department.\nA: \n\nQ: Employees earning more than their manager.\nA: \n\nQ: Top product category by revenue per month.\nA: "},"intuitive":"<p>Level 2 asks &quot;who is the best in each group?&quot;. The trick is to give every row a rank inside its own group (like ranking students in each class separately) and then keep only ranks you want. The only real decision is what to do with ties: if two students tie for 2nd place, do you want both to count as &quot;top 3&quot;? Say your tie rule out loud; interviewers love it.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Top-N per group appears in almost every SQL round.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Rank inside each group with PARTITION BY, filter in an outer query, and state the tie rule.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;Top-N per group is a ranking problem. I rank rows inside each group with a window function partitioned by the group and ordered by the measure, then filter on the rank in an outer query or CTE. The decision is ties. DENSE_RANK with rank at most three keeps everyone tied, so a department can return four rows. ROW_NUMBER returns exactly three but picks among ties arbitrarily unless I add a tie-breaker. RANK can skip the third place. Employees earning more than their manager is a self join. The catch: I say my tie rule out loud, and I compute revenue from order items, not from the order total after a join.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Top two salaries per department, traced","html":"<p>Return the two highest salaries in each department, keeping ties.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Rank inside each department</strong> with <code>DENSE_RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY dept_id ORDER BY salary DESC) AS r</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>The ranked rows.</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>dept</th><th>name</th><th>salary</th><th>r</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sales</td><td>Asha</td><td>90,000</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Sales</td><td>Ravi</td><td>70,000</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>Sales</td><td>Meera</td><td>70,000</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>Sales</td><td>Karan</td><td>50,000</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>Data</td><td>Isha</td><td>80,000</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Data</td><td>Dev</td><td>60,000</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>Data</td><td>Nina</td><td>60,000</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>Ops</td><td>Zoya</td><td>95,000</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Ops</td><td>Omar</td><td>55,000</td><td>2</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ol start=\"3\">\n<li><strong>Filter outside:</strong> window functions cannot be used in WHERE, so wrap the query: <code>SELECT * FROM (...) t WHERE r &lt;= 2</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Result:</strong> 8 rows. Sales returns 3 people (the tie at 70,000), Data returns 3, Ops returns 2.</li>\n<li><strong>Compare with ROW_NUMBER:</strong> exactly 2 rows per department, 6 in total, and one of the tied people is dropped at random. State which behaviour you want before writing the query.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Top-N per group is always &quot;rank in an inner query, filter in the outer query&quot;. The only real decision is how ties are handled.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-results-on-the-sample-data","text":"Results on the sample data","level":3},{"id":"h-the-shapes-behind-each-problem","text":"The shapes behind each problem","level":3},{"id":"h-more-problems-with-solutions","text":"More problems, with solutions","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"qa","id":"2.5#5","src":"c","q":"Nth highest salary (overall).","a":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">DISTINCT</span> salary <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> (\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> salary, <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">DENSE_RANK</span>() <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> salary <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">DESC</span>) r <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> employees\n) t <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> r <span class=\"hljs-operator\">=</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>;       <span class=\"hljs-comment\">-- change 2 to N</span></code></pre></div>\n","s":"<code>DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY salary DESC)</code> and pick rank N, using <code>DISTINCT</code> for the value.","label":"P5"},{"k":"qa","id":"2.5#6","src":"c","q":"Top 3 earners in each department.","a":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WITH</span> ranked <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> (\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> e.<span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span>, <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">DENSE_RANK</span>() <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> dept_id <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> salary <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">DESC</span>) r\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> employees e\n)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> ranked <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> r <span class=\"hljs-operator\">&lt;=</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>;</code></pre></div>\n","s":"Rank within <code>PARTITION BY dept_id</code>, then keep rank &lt;= 3 in an outer query.","label":"P6"},{"k":"qa","id":"2.5#7","src":"c","q":"Employees earning more than their manager.","a":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> e.name\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> employees e <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">JOIN</span> employees m <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ON</span> e.manager_id <span class=\"hljs-operator\">=</span> m.emp_id\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> e.salary <span class=\"hljs-operator\">&gt;</span> m.salary;</code></pre></div>\n","s":"Self join the table: employee <code>e</code> joined to manager <code>m</code> on <code>e.manager_id = m.emp_id</code>, compare salaries.","label":"P7"},{"k":"qa","id":"2.5#8","src":"c","q":"Top product category by revenue per month.","a":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WITH</span> monthly <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> (\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> DATE_TRUNC(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;month&#x27;</span>, o.order_date) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> mth, p.category,\n         <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">SUM</span>(oi.qty <span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span> oi.price) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> rev\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> orders o\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">JOIN</span> order_items oi <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">USING</span> (order_id)\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">JOIN</span> products p <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">USING</span> (product_id)\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>\n), ranked <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> (\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span>, <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">RANK</span>() <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> mth <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> rev <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">DESC</span>) r <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> monthly\n)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> mth, category, rev <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> ranked <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> r <span class=\"hljs-operator\">=</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>;</code></pre></div>\n","s":"Sum revenue by month and category, rank categories inside each month, keep rank 1.","label":"P8"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-results-on-the-sample-data\">Results on the sample data</h3>\n<p><strong>P5, Nth highest salary (N = 2):</strong> 90,000 (Zoya earns 95,000 and is first; Asha&#39;s 90,000 is second).</p>\n<p><strong>P6, top 3 per department</strong> with <code>DENSE_RANK</code> (ties included):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>name</th><th>dept_id</th><th>salary</th><th>rank</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Asha</td><td>10</td><td>90000</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Meera</td><td>10</td><td>70000</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>Ravi</td><td>10</td><td>70000</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>Karan</td><td>10</td><td>50000</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>Isha</td><td>20</td><td>80000</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Dev</td><td>20</td><td>60000</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>Nina</td><td>20</td><td>60000</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>Zoya</td><td>30</td><td>95000</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Omar</td><td>30</td><td>55000</td><td>2</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Department 10 returns four rows: the tie at rank 2 means the third distinct salary (Karan, rank 3) still qualifies. With <code>ROW_NUMBER &lt;= 3</code> you would get exactly three, choosing between Ravi and Meera arbitrarily; with <code>RANK &lt;= 3</code> you would drop Karan, whose <code>RANK</code> is 4.</p>\n<p><strong>P7, earning more than their manager:</strong> Zoya (95,000, managed by Omar on 55,000).</p>\n<p><strong>P8, top category by revenue per month:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>month</th><th>category</th><th>revenue</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>2025-01</td><td>Electronics</td><td>7000</td></tr><tr><td>2025-02</td><td>Fashion</td><td>3900</td></tr><tr><td>2025-03</td><td>Electronics</td><td>4000</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"h-the-shapes-behind-each-problem\">The shapes behind each problem</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>P5</strong> <code>DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY salary DESC)</code> then <code>WHERE r = N</code>. The outer <code>DISTINCT</code> returns the value once when several people share it. Without window functions: <code>SELECT MAX(salary) FROM e WHERE salary &lt; (SELECT MAX(salary) FROM e)</code> for N = 2.</li>\n<li><strong>P6</strong> rank inside <code>PARTITION BY dept_id</code>, keep <code>r &lt;= 3</code> in an outer query or CTE.</li>\n<li><strong>P7</strong> a <strong>self join</strong>: one copy of <code>employees</code> plays the employee (<code>e</code>) and the other the manager (<code>m</code>), joined on <code>e.manager_id = m.emp_id</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>P8</strong> two levels of aggregation and ranking: a CTE to total revenue by month and category, a second CTE to rank categories within each month, then keep rank 1. Note the <strong>three-table join</strong> <code>orders → order_items → products</code>, and that revenue comes from <code>qty * price</code>, not from <code>orders.amount</code> (which would be multiplied by fan-out).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"h-more-problems-with-solutions\">More problems, with solutions</h3>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m2-level-2~1","q":"Return each user&#39;s most recent order (exactly one row per user).","a":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> user_id, order_id, order_date\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> (\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> o.<span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span>, <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">ROW_NUMBER</span>() <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> user_id <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> order_date <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">DESC</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> rn\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> orders o\n) t\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> rn <span class=\"hljs-operator\">=</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>;</code></pre></div>\n<p>Here <code>ROW_NUMBER</code> is right because you want exactly one row. If two orders could share a timestamp, add a tie-breaker: <code>ORDER BY order_date DESC, order_id DESC</code>.</p>\n","s":"<code>ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY user_id ORDER BY order_date DESC)</code>, keep <code>rn = 1</code>."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m2-level-2~2","q":"Top 2 products by revenue within each category.","a":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WITH</span> rev <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> (\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> p.category, p.product_name, <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">SUM</span>(oi.qty <span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span> oi.price) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> revenue\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> order_items oi <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">JOIN</span> products p <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">USING</span> (product_id)\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> p.category, p.product_name\n), ranked <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> (\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span>, <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">DENSE_RANK</span>() <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> category <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> revenue <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">DESC</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> rk <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> rev\n)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> ranked <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> rk <span class=\"hljs-operator\">&lt;=</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>;</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>category</th><th>product_name</th><th>revenue</th><th>rk</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Electronics</td><td>Earbuds</td><td>8900</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Electronics</td><td>Power bank</td><td>2100</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>Fashion</td><td>Sneakers</td><td>4800</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Fashion</td><td>T-shirt</td><td>2500</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>Grocery</td><td>Basmati rice</td><td>1800</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Grocery</td><td>Tea</td><td>1400</td><td>2</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n","s":"Aggregate revenue per product, then <code>DENSE_RANK</code> within category and keep rank &lt;= 2."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m2-level-2~3","q":"List employees who earn more than the average salary of their own department.","a":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> name, dept_id, salary\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> e.<span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span>, <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">AVG</span>(salary) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> dept_id) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> dept_avg <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> employees e) t\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> salary <span class=\"hljs-operator\">&gt;</span> dept_avg;</code></pre></div>\n<p>Returns Asha (Sales), Isha (Data) and Zoya (Ops). The window average is available on every row without a join, which is why this pattern beats a <code>JOIN</code> to a grouped subquery for readability.</p>\n","s":"Compute the department average as a window function and compare in an outer query."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m2-level-2~ca0_1","q":"Top 3 salaries per department: when does DENSE_RANK return more than 3 rows per department?","a":"<p>When salaries tie at a rank, for example 90, 70, 70, 50 returns four rows because 50 has dense rank 3.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m2-level-2~ca0_2","q":"Latest order per user, exactly one row each: which function?","a":"<p><code>ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY user_id ORDER BY order_date DESC)</code> with rn = 1 (add a tie-breaker).</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m2-level-2~ca0_3","q":"Employees earning more than their manager: which technique?","a":"<p>Self join: employee e joined to manager m on <code>e.manager_id = m.emp_id</code>, then compare salaries.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m2-level-2~ta1_1","q":"<p>Which employees earn more than their own manager in the practice data? Write the query and give the answer. (Zoya&#39;s manager is Omar; Isha&#39;s manager is Asha.)</p>\n","a":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> e.name, e.salary, m.name <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> manager, m.salary <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> manager_salary\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> employees e\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">JOIN</span> employees m <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ON</span> e.manager_id <span class=\"hljs-operator\">=</span> m.emp_id\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> e.salary <span class=\"hljs-operator\">&gt;</span> m.salary;</code></pre></div>\n<p>Only Zoya: 95,000 against Omar&#39;s 55,000. Isha earns 80,000 and her manager Asha earns 90,000. This is a self-join: the same table appears twice with two aliases, one in the role of employee and one in the role of manager.</p>\n"}]},"m2-level-3":{"id":"m2-level-3","slug":"sql-problems-level-3-running-totals-growth-lag-lead","m":2,"title":"SQL problems, level 3: running totals, growth, LAG/LEAD","min":35,"type":"practice","priority":"Highest","prereq":["m2-level-2"],"tags":["sql","practice","window-functions"],"qa":11,"ai":{"scope":"Running totals, moving averages, growth percentages and gaps between consecutive events using window frames, LAG and LEAD.","keyTerms":["running total","moving average","LAG","month-over-month growth","NULLIF","frame clause"],"interviewQs":["Compute a running total of daily revenue.","Compute a 7-day moving average. What is the catch when days are missing?","Compute month-over-month growth and avoid division by zero."],"mistakes":["Computing a moving average over rows when the requirement is over days","Forgetting NULLIF and dividing by zero","Using integer division for percentages"],"practice":"Reproduce P9 to P12 on the sample data and check each number by hand.","related":["m2-ranking","m2-level-4a"],"points":"Q: Running total of daily revenue.\nA: \n\nQ: 7-day moving average.\nA: \n\nQ: Month-over-month growth %.\nA: \n\nQ: Days between a user's consecutive orders.\nA: "},"intuitive":"<p>Level 3 is about time: &quot;how much so far?&quot;, &quot;what is the average of the last few days?&quot;, &quot;how much did it grow compared with last month?&quot;. A running total is a bank balance that grows with each deposit. A moving average smooths out bumpy days by looking at a few neighbours. Month-over-month growth compares this month with the previous row using LAG, which means &quot;peek at the row just above me&quot;.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Growth and trend questions are everywhere in product and analytics interviews.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> SUM() OVER for running totals, AVG() OVER with a frame for moving averages, LAG for comparisons.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;Time-based questions use window functions over ordered rows. A running total is SUM OVER ordered by date; when I aggregate by day first it is SUM of SUM OVER. A moving average uses AVG with a frame such as six rows preceding to current row, but that counts rows, so missing days need a calendar table. Previous value comparisons use LAG, partitioned by user or category. Month-over-month growth is current minus previous, divided by previous, with NULLIF to avoid dividing by zero and a decimal multiplier to avoid integer division. The catch: first rows have no previous value and return NULL, and I must partition LAG per entity.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Running total and month-over-month change, traced","html":"<p>Delivered revenue by month in the practice data: January 7,800; February 5,200; March 8,100.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>First reduce to one row per month</strong> (aggregate before the window):</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WITH</span> m <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> (\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> DATE_TRUNC(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;month&#x27;</span>, order_date) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">month</span>, <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">SUM</span>(amount) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> rev\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> orders <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> status <span class=\"hljs-operator\">=</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;delivered&#x27;</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">month</span>, rev,\n  <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">SUM</span>(rev) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">month</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">running</span>,\n  <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">LAG</span>(rev) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">month</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> prev,\n  <span class=\"hljs-number\">100.0</span> <span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span> (rev <span class=\"hljs-operator\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">LAG</span>(rev) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">month</span>))\n        <span class=\"hljs-operator\">/</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">NULLIF</span>(<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">LAG</span>(rev) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">month</span>), <span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> pct\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> m;</code></pre></div>\n<ol start=\"2\">\n<li><strong>Trace each window column.</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>month</th><th>rev</th><th>running</th><th>prev (LAG)</th><th>change</th><th>% change</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Jan</td><td>7,800</td><td>7,800</td><td>NULL</td><td>NULL</td><td>NULL</td></tr><tr><td>Feb</td><td>5,200</td><td>13,000</td><td>7,800</td><td>−2,600</td><td>−33.3%</td></tr><tr><td>Mar</td><td>8,100</td><td>21,100</td><td>5,200</td><td>+2,900</td><td>+55.8%</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ol start=\"3\">\n<li><strong>Running total:</strong> each row adds its revenue to everything before it in month order.</li>\n<li><strong>LAG:</strong> looks one row back. January has no previous row, so it is NULL, and so is its change. That is correct, not an error.</li>\n<li><strong>Percent change:</strong> (8,100 − 5,200) / 5,200 = 55.8%. The <code>100.0</code> makes the division decimal, and <code>NULLIF</code> avoids dividing by zero.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Window functions answer &quot;compared with before&quot; questions while keeping one row per month. Aggregate to the grain you want first, then apply the window.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-results-on-the-sample-data","text":"Results on the sample data","level":3},{"id":"h-details-worth-saying-out-loud","text":"Details worth saying out loud","level":3},{"id":"h-more-problems-with-solutions","text":"More problems, with solutions","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"qa","id":"2.5#9","src":"c","q":"Running total of daily revenue.","a":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> order_date,\n       <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">SUM</span>(amount) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> daily_rev,\n       <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">SUM</span>(<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">SUM</span>(amount)) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> order_date) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> running_total\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> orders\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> order_date;</code></pre></div>\n","s":"<code>SUM(SUM(amount)) OVER (ORDER BY order_date)</code>: aggregate per day, then a window sum over the daily totals.","label":"P9"},{"k":"qa","id":"2.5#10","src":"c","q":"7-day moving average.","a":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> order_date, daily_rev,\n       <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">AVG</span>(daily_rev) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> order_date <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ROWS</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BETWEEN</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">6</span> PRECEDING <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AND</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">CURRENT</span> <span class=\"hljs-type\">ROW</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> ma7\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> order_date, <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">SUM</span>(amount) daily_rev <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> orders <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> order_date) t;</code></pre></div>\n","s":"<code>AVG(daily_rev) OVER (ORDER BY day ROWS BETWEEN 6 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW)</code>; beware missing days.","label":"P10"},{"k":"qa","id":"2.5#11","src":"c","q":"Month-over-month growth %.","a":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WITH</span> m <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> (\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> DATE_TRUNC(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;month&#x27;</span>, order_date) mth, <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">SUM</span>(amount) rev\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> orders <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>\n)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> mth, rev,\n       <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">LAG</span>(rev) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> mth) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> prev_rev,\n       ROUND(<span class=\"hljs-number\">100.0</span> <span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span> (rev <span class=\"hljs-operator\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">LAG</span>(rev) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> mth)) <span class=\"hljs-operator\">/</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">NULLIF</span>(<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">LAG</span>(rev) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> mth),<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>), <span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> mom_pct\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> m;</code></pre></div>\n","s":"<code>(rev - LAG(rev)) / NULLIF(LAG(rev), 0) * 100</code> ordered by month.","label":"P11"},{"k":"qa","id":"2.5#12","src":"c","q":"Days between a user&#39;s consecutive orders.","a":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> user_id, order_date,\n       order_date <span class=\"hljs-operator\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">LAG</span>(order_date) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> user_id <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> order_date) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> gap_days\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> orders;</code></pre></div>\n","s":"<code>order_date - LAG(order_date) OVER (PARTITION BY user_id ORDER BY order_date)</code>.","label":"P12"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-results-on-the-sample-data\">Results on the sample data</h3>\n<p><strong>P9, running total of daily revenue</strong> (first six days):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>order_date</th><th>daily_rev</th><th>running_total</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>2025-01-05</td><td>1500</td><td>1500</td></tr><tr><td>2025-01-06</td><td>2500</td><td>4000</td></tr><tr><td>2025-01-12</td><td>3000</td><td>7000</td></tr><tr><td>2025-01-15</td><td>400</td><td>7400</td></tr><tr><td>2025-01-20</td><td>800</td><td>8200</td></tr><tr><td>2025-02-03</td><td>1200</td><td>9400</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><code>SUM(SUM(amount)) OVER (ORDER BY order_date)</code> looks odd but is correct: the inner <code>SUM</code> is the <code>GROUP BY</code> aggregate (daily revenue); the outer is the window function summing those daily totals.</p>\n<p><strong>P11, month-over-month growth:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>month</th><th>revenue</th><th>prev_rev</th><th>mom_pct</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>2025-01</td><td>8200</td><td>NULL</td><td>NULL</td></tr><tr><td>2025-02</td><td>5200</td><td>8200</td><td>-36.59</td></tr><tr><td>2025-03</td><td>8100</td><td>5200</td><td>55.77</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>P12, days between consecutive orders</strong> (users 1 and 2): user 1 waited 15, 21 and 20 days between orders; user 2 waited 39 and 19 days. The first order of each user has <code>NULL</code> because there is no previous row.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-details-worth-saying-out-loud\">Details worth saying out loud</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Frame for a moving average.</strong> <code>ROWS BETWEEN 6 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW</code> means <em>the current row and the six rows before it</em>, which is 7 <strong>rows</strong>. If some days have no orders, those rows do not exist, so the &quot;7 rows&quot; stretch over more than 7 <strong>days</strong>. To get a true 7-day average, first build a calendar (one row per date, filled with 0 revenue) and then apply the window, or use a <code>RANGE BETWEEN INTERVAL &#39;6 days&#39; PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW</code> frame where your engine supports it.</li>\n<li><strong>The first window is short.</strong> On day 1 the average uses only one value. Decide whether to show it or hide rows with fewer than 7 observations.</li>\n<li><strong>Percent growth</strong> = <code>(current - previous) / previous</code>. Protect against zero with <code>NULLIF(previous, 0)</code> and avoid integer division by multiplying by <code>100.0</code>.</li>\n<li><strong><code>LAG</code> per group</strong> needs <code>PARTITION BY</code>: <code>LAG(order_date) OVER (PARTITION BY user_id ORDER BY order_date)</code>. Without it, the first order of user 2 would be compared to the last order of user 1.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"h-more-problems-with-solutions\">More problems, with solutions</h3>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m2-level-3~1","q":"For each user, show the gap in days between their first and second order.","a":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WITH</span> o <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> (\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> user_id, order_date,\n         <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">ROW_NUMBER</span>() <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> user_id <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> order_date) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> rn,\n         <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">LAG</span>(order_date) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> user_id <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> order_date) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> prev\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> orders\n)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> user_id, prev <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> first_order, order_date <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> second_order,\n       order_date <span class=\"hljs-operator\">-</span> prev <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> days_to_second          <span class=\"hljs-comment\">-- Postgres; use DATEDIFF / julianday elsewhere</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> o <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> rn <span class=\"hljs-operator\">=</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>;</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>user_id</th><th>first_order</th><th>second_order</th><th>days_to_second</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>2025-01-05</td><td>2025-01-20</td><td>15</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>2025-01-06</td><td>2025-02-14</td><td>39</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>2025-01-15</td><td>2025-02-03</td><td>19</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>2025-01-12</td><td>2025-03-15</td><td>62</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>2025-02-05</td><td>2025-03-09</td><td>32</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>2025-02-20</td><td>2025-03-18</td><td>26</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Users with only one order (user 7) simply do not appear. This measure (time to repeat purchase) is a common retention metric.</p>\n","s":"<code>LAG(order_date)</code> over each user&#39;s ordered orders, keep the row where <code>ROW_NUMBER = 2</code>."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m2-level-3~2","q":"Cumulative spend per user over time, restarting for each user.","a":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> user_id, order_date, amount,\n       <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">SUM</span>(amount) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> user_id <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> order_date) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> cumulative\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> orders;</code></pre></div>\n<p>For user 1 the cumulative column reads 1500, 2300, 2900, 5100. Without <code>PARTITION BY</code> the total would run across all users.</p>\n","s":"Add <code>PARTITION BY user_id</code> to the running-sum window."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m2-level-3~3","q":"What share of its month&#39;s revenue does each order contribute?","a":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> order_id, amount,\n       ROUND(<span class=\"hljs-number\">100.0</span> <span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span> amount <span class=\"hljs-operator\">/</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">SUM</span>(amount) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> DATE_TRUNC(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;month&#x27;</span>, order_date)), <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> pct_of_month\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> orders;</code></pre></div>\n<p>For January (total 8,200): order 6 contributes 36.6%, order 3 contributes 30.5%, order 1 contributes 18.3%, and so on. No join or subquery is needed because the window supplies the group total on every row.</p>\n","s":"Divide each amount by <code>SUM(amount) OVER (PARTITION BY month)</code>."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m2-level-3~ca0_1","q":"Why can a &quot;7-day moving average&quot; using <code>ROWS BETWEEN 6 PRECEDING</code> be wrong?","a":"<p>It averages 7 rows, not 7 days; days with no data are missing. Build a calendar with zero-filled days first.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m2-level-3~ca0_2","q":"Days between consecutive orders per user: what is the expression?","a":"<p><code>order_date - LAG(order_date) OVER (PARTITION BY user_id ORDER BY order_date)</code>.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m2-level-3~ca0_3","q":"Revenue 8,200 then 5,200. Month-over-month growth?","a":"<p>(5,200 - 8,200) / 8,200 = -36.6%.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m2-level-3~ta1_1","q":"<p>User 1&#39;s orders are dated 5 January, 20 January, 10 February and 2 March. Using <code>order_date - LAG(order_date) OVER (PARTITION BY user_id ORDER BY order_date)</code>, what are the gaps in days, and what is the average gap?</p>\n","a":"<p>First order: NULL (nothing before it). Then 15 days (5 to 20 January), 21 days (20 January to 10 February), and 20 days (10 February to 2 March). The average of the three gaps is 56 / 3 ≈ 18.7 days. <code>PARTITION BY user_id</code> makes LAG look back only within the same user; without it, the previous row could belong to someone else.</p>\n"}]},"m2-level-4a":{"id":"m2-level-4a","slug":"sql-problems-level-4a-streaks-retention-cohorts","m":2,"title":"SQL problems, level 4a: streaks, retention, cohorts","min":40,"type":"practice","priority":"Highest","prereq":["m2-level-3"],"tags":["sql","practice","retention","cohorts"],"qa":9,"ai":{"scope":"Consecutive-day streaks with the gaps-and-islands technique, day-N retention by cohort, and monthly cohort retention tables.","keyTerms":["gaps and islands","streak","row number trick","day-N retention","cohort table","LEAD","DISTINCT"],"interviewQs":["Find users with logins on three or more consecutive days.","Compute D1 and D7 retention by signup cohort.","Build a monthly cohort retention table. How do you read it?"],"mistakes":["Forgetting DISTINCT so duplicate logins break the streak arithmetic","Dividing by activity rows instead of cohort size","Counting a user as retained on any later day instead of exactly day N"],"practice":"Write the streak query and the D1/D7 query from memory on the sample data and compare with the results here.","related":["m2-level-3","m2-level-4b","m13-metrics"],"points":"Q: Users with logins on 3 or more consecutive days (gaps-and-islands).\nA: Idea: subtracting row number from a consecutive date gives a constant per streak.\n\nAlternative using LEAD:\n\nQ: Day-N retention (D1, D7) by signup cohort.\nA: \n\nQ: Monthly cohort retention table.\nA: "},"intuitive":"<p>A streak is &quot;days in a row&quot;. Here is the trick: number the days 1, 2, 3 for each user, and subtract that number from each date. Dates that are consecutive move together with the counter, so the subtraction gives the same answer for the whole streak; a gap makes the answer jump. That &quot;same answer&quot; becomes a label for the streak, so you can group by it and count. Retention just asks: of the people who joined on a day, how many came back exactly 1 day (or 7 days) later?</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Retention and streak questions are favourites at product companies because they mirror real growth metrics.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Streaks: date minus row number. Retention: cohort size as the denominator, return on exactly day N.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;For streaks I use gaps and islands. After keeping one row per user per day, I number each user&#39;s days 1, 2, 3 and subtract that number from the date. Consecutive days produce the same result, so that value labels a streak; I group by it and use the count as the length. For retention, I define the cohort as each user&#39;s first day, then count users who return exactly one or seven days later, divided by cohort size, using a left join so non-returners stay in. A monthly cohort table is the same idea by month. The catch: duplicates break the streak arithmetic, and I name which retention definition I used.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Find login streaks with \"date minus row number\"","html":"<p>User 1 logged in on 2, 3, 3 (twice), 4, 6 and 7 January. What are the streaks of consecutive days?</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Remove duplicates first:</strong> 2, 3, 4, 6, 7 January. A duplicate row would break the trick.</li>\n<li><strong>Number the days in order</strong> with <code>ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY user_id ORDER BY login_date)</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Subtract the row number from the date.</strong> Consecutive dates rise by 1 and so does the row number, so the difference stays constant within a streak.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>login_date</th><th>row number</th><th>date − row number</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>2 Jan</td><td>1</td><td>1 Jan</td></tr><tr><td>3 Jan</td><td>2</td><td>1 Jan</td></tr><tr><td>4 Jan</td><td>3</td><td>1 Jan</td></tr><tr><td>6 Jan</td><td>4</td><td>2 Jan</td></tr><tr><td>7 Jan</td><td>5</td><td>2 Jan</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ol start=\"4\">\n<li><strong>Group by that constant.</strong> Group &quot;1 Jan&quot; covers 2 to 4 January: a streak of 3 days. Group &quot;2 Jan&quot; covers 6 to 7 January: 2 days.</li>\n<li><strong>Answer:</strong> user 1&#39;s longest streak is 3 days. The gap on 5 January changed the constant, which is exactly how a break is detected.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> The constant is not a real date. It is a label that stays the same while the days are consecutive. Group by it to turn rows into streaks.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-p13-how-date-minus-row-number-works","text":"P13: how &quot;date minus row number&quot; works","level":3},{"id":"h-p14-day-n-retention","text":"P14: day-N retention","level":3},{"id":"h-p15-the-monthly-cohort-table","text":"P15: the monthly cohort table","level":3},{"id":"h-more-problems-with-solutions","text":"More problems, with solutions","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"qa","id":"2.5#13","src":"c","q":"Users with logins on 3 or more consecutive days (gaps-and-islands).","a":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WITH</span> d <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> (\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">DISTINCT</span> user_id, login_date <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> logins\n), grp <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> (\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> user_id, login_date,\n         login_date <span class=\"hljs-operator\">-</span> (<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">ROW_NUMBER</span>() <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> user_id <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> login_date))::<span class=\"hljs-type\">int</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> g\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> d\n)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> user_id, <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">MIN</span>(login_date) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> streak_start, <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">MAX</span>(login_date) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> streak_end, <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">COUNT</span>(<span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> streak_len\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> grp\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> user_id, g\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">HAVING</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">COUNT</span>(<span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span>) <span class=\"hljs-operator\">&gt;=</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>;</code></pre></div>\n<p>Idea: subtracting row number from a consecutive date gives a constant per streak.</p>\n<p>Alternative using LEAD:</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">DISTINCT</span> user_id <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> (\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> user_id, login_date,\n         <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">LEAD</span>(login_date, <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> user_id <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> login_date) d1,\n         <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">LEAD</span>(login_date, <span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> user_id <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> login_date) d2\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">DISTINCT</span> user_id, login_date <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> logins) x\n) t <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> d1 <span class=\"hljs-operator\">=</span> login_date <span class=\"hljs-operator\">+</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AND</span> d2 <span class=\"hljs-operator\">=</span> login_date <span class=\"hljs-operator\">+</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>;</code></pre></div>\n","s":"Dedupe to user-days, <code>login_date − ROW_NUMBER()</code> is constant within a streak; group by it and keep length &gt;= 3.","label":"P13"},{"k":"qa","id":"2.5#14","src":"c","q":"Day-N retention (D1, D7) by signup cohort.","a":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WITH</span> first_day <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> (\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> user_id, <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">MIN</span>(login_date) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> d0 <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> logins <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> user_id\n)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> f.d0 <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> cohort_date,\n       <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">COUNT</span>(<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">DISTINCT</span> f.user_id) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> cohort_size,\n       <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">COUNT</span>(<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">DISTINCT</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">CASE</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHEN</span> l.login_date <span class=\"hljs-operator\">=</span> f.d0 <span class=\"hljs-operator\">+</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">THEN</span> l.user_id <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">END</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> d1_users,\n       ROUND(<span class=\"hljs-number\">100.0</span> <span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">COUNT</span>(<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">DISTINCT</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">CASE</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHEN</span> l.login_date <span class=\"hljs-operator\">=</span> f.d0 <span class=\"hljs-operator\">+</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">THEN</span> l.user_id <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">END</span>)\n             <span class=\"hljs-operator\">/</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">COUNT</span>(<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">DISTINCT</span> f.user_id), <span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> d1_retention_pct,\n       ROUND(<span class=\"hljs-number\">100.0</span> <span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">COUNT</span>(<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">DISTINCT</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">CASE</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHEN</span> l.login_date <span class=\"hljs-operator\">=</span> f.d0 <span class=\"hljs-operator\">+</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">7</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">THEN</span> l.user_id <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">END</span>)\n             <span class=\"hljs-operator\">/</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">COUNT</span>(<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">DISTINCT</span> f.user_id), <span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> d7_retention_pct\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> first_day f\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">LEFT</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">JOIN</span> logins l <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ON</span> l.user_id <span class=\"hljs-operator\">=</span> f.user_id\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> f.d0\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> f.d0;</code></pre></div>\n","s":"Cohort = first login day; count users returning exactly on day +1 and +7, divided by cohort size.","label":"P14"},{"k":"qa","id":"2.5#15","src":"c","q":"Monthly cohort retention table.","a":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WITH</span> cohort <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> (\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> user_id, DATE_TRUNC(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;month&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">MIN</span>(order_date)) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> cohort_month\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> orders <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> user_id\n), activity <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> (\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">DISTINCT</span> user_id, DATE_TRUNC(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;month&#x27;</span>, order_date) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> act_month <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> orders\n)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> c.cohort_month,\n       (<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">EXTRACT</span>(<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">YEAR</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> a.act_month) <span class=\"hljs-operator\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">EXTRACT</span>(<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">YEAR</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> c.cohort_month)) <span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">12</span>\n       <span class=\"hljs-operator\">+</span> (<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">EXTRACT</span>(<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">MONTH</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> a.act_month) <span class=\"hljs-operator\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">EXTRACT</span>(<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">MONTH</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> c.cohort_month)) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> month_number,\n       <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">COUNT</span>(<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">DISTINCT</span> a.user_id) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> active_users\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> cohort c <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">JOIN</span> activity a <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">USING</span> (user_id)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>;</code></pre></div>\n","s":"Cohort month from each user&#39;s first order, month_number = months between activity and cohort, count distinct users.","label":"P15"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-p13-how-date-minus-row-number-works\">P13: how &quot;date minus row number&quot; works</h3>\n<p>User 1&#39;s distinct login days, numbered in order:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>login_date</th><th>rn</th><th>login_date − rn days = g</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>2025-01-02</td><td>1</td><td>2025-01-01</td></tr><tr><td>2025-01-03</td><td>2</td><td>2025-01-01</td></tr><tr><td>2025-01-04</td><td>3</td><td>2025-01-01</td></tr><tr><td>2025-01-06</td><td>4</td><td>2025-01-02</td></tr><tr><td>2025-01-07</td><td>5</td><td>2025-01-02</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The first three days share <code>g = 2025-01-01</code> (one island), then the gap between the 4th and the 6th moves <code>g</code> to <code>2025-01-02</code> (a new island). <code>GROUP BY user_id, g</code> gives each streak; <code>MIN</code>/<code>MAX</code> give its start and end, <code>COUNT(*)</code> its length.</p>\n<p>Results on the sample data (streaks of at least 3 days):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>user_id</th><th>streak_start</th><th>streak_end</th><th>streak_len</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>2025-01-02</td><td>2025-01-04</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>2025-01-05</td><td>2025-01-10</td><td>6</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>2025-01-10</td><td>2025-01-13</td><td>4</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<aside class=\"callout trap\"><div class=\"callout-t\">DISTINCT first</div><div class=\"callout-b\"><p>User 1 has a duplicate login row for 2025-01-03. Without the <code>SELECT DISTINCT user_id, login_date</code> step, <code>ROW_NUMBER</code> would count both rows, the subtraction would drift by one, and the streak would split wrongly. Always de-duplicate to one row per user per day before the trick.</p>\n</div></aside>"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<p>Dialect note: <code>login_date - rn</code> works in PostgreSQL because date minus integer is a date. In MySQL use <code>DATE_SUB(login_date, INTERVAL rn DAY)</code>; in SQLite <code>date(login_date, &#39;-&#39; || rn || &#39; days&#39;)</code>.</p>\n<p>The <code>LEAD</code> alternative (check that the next two rows are +1 and +2 days) is easy to explain but only tests &quot;at least 3 in a row&quot; and does not scale to &quot;at least 30 in a row&quot;; the island method does.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-p14-day-n-retention\">P14: day-N retention</h3>\n<p>Define the <strong>cohort</strong> as the first day a user logged in (<code>MIN(login_date)</code> per user), then join back to <code>logins</code> and count who returned exactly 1 and 7 days later. On the sample data:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>cohort_date</th><th>cohort_size</th><th>d1_users</th><th>d1_retention_pct</th><th>d7_retention_pct</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>2025-01-02</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>100.0</td><td>0.0</td></tr><tr><td>2025-01-03</td><td>2</td><td>1</td><td>50.0</td><td>50.0</td></tr><tr><td>2025-01-10</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>100.0</td><td>0.0</td></tr><tr><td>2025-02-01</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0.0</td><td>0.0</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Read it like this: of the two users whose first login was 3 January, one came back on the 4th (D1 = 50%) and one came back on the 10th (D7 = 50%). Key design decisions to say out loud:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Denominator</strong> is the cohort size (<code>COUNT(DISTINCT f.user_id)</code>), not the number of login rows.</li>\n<li><strong>Exactly day N</strong> (<code>login_date = d0 + 7</code>) is &quot;D7 retention&quot;. &quot;Within 7 days&quot; or &quot;on or after day 7&quot; are different metrics (rolling retention); name which one you computed.</li>\n<li><code>LEFT JOIN</code> keeps cohort users who never returned. An inner join would silently drop them and inflate the percentages.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"h-p15-the-monthly-cohort-table\">P15: the monthly cohort table</h3>\n<p>Result on the sample orders (month_number 0 is the first purchase month):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>cohort_month</th><th>month_number</th><th>active_users</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>2025-01</td><td>0</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>2025-01</td><td>1</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>2025-01</td><td>2</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>2025-02</td><td>0</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>2025-02</td><td>1</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>2025-03</td><td>0</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Pivoted into the usual triangle and divided by the month-0 size:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Cohort</th><th>M0</th><th>M1</th><th>M2</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Jan (4 users)</td><td>100%</td><td>75%</td><td>75%</td></tr><tr><td>Feb (2 users)</td><td>100%</td><td>100%</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Mar (1 user)</td><td>100%</td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Each <strong>row</strong> is a cohort, each <strong>column</strong> is months since joining, so you can compare how well each cohort retains at the same age. The triangle shape appears because newer cohorts have not had time to reach later months. Watch for <strong>small cohorts</strong> (the March cohort has one person, so its 100% means little) and always show cohort sizes.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-more-problems-with-solutions\">More problems, with solutions</h3>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m2-level-4a~1","q":"Find each user&#39;s longest login streak.","a":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WITH</span> d <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">DISTINCT</span> user_id, login_date <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> logins),\ngrp <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> (\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> user_id, login_date,\n         login_date <span class=\"hljs-operator\">-</span> (<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">ROW_NUMBER</span>() <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> user_id <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> login_date))::<span class=\"hljs-type\">int</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> g\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> d\n),\nstreaks <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> user_id, g, <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">COUNT</span>(<span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> len <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> grp <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> user_id, g)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> user_id, <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">MAX</span>(len) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> longest_streak <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> streaks <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> user_id;</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>user_id</th><th>longest_streak</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>6</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Two levels of grouping: first by island, then by user.</p>\n","s":"Build the islands, count the length of each, then take <code>MAX(len)</code> per user."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m2-level-4a~2","q":"For each month, how many buyers are new (first ever order that month) and how many are returning?","a":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WITH</span> first_m <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> (\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> user_id, DATE_TRUNC(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;month&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">MIN</span>(order_date)) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> fm <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> orders <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> user_id\n)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> DATE_TRUNC(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;month&#x27;</span>, o.order_date) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">month</span>,\n       <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">COUNT</span>(<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">DISTINCT</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">CASE</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHEN</span> f.fm <span class=\"hljs-operator\">=</span>  DATE_TRUNC(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;month&#x27;</span>, o.order_date) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">THEN</span> o.user_id <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">END</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> new_buyers,\n       <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">COUNT</span>(<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">DISTINCT</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">CASE</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHEN</span> f.fm <span class=\"hljs-operator\">&lt;</span>  DATE_TRUNC(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;month&#x27;</span>, o.order_date) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">THEN</span> o.user_id <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">END</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> returning_buyers\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> orders o <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">JOIN</span> first_m f <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">USING</span> (user_id)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>;</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>month</th><th>new_buyers</th><th>returning_buyers</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>2025-01</td><td>4</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>2025-02</td><td>2</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>2025-03</td><td>1</td><td>5</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Conditional <code>COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN ...)</code> is the workhorse of product analytics SQL.</p>\n","s":"Compute each user&#39;s first-order month, then compare it with the month of each order."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m2-level-4a~ca0_1","q":"Why must you de-duplicate login rows before the row-number trick?","a":"<p>A duplicate day gets its own row number, shifting the subtraction and splitting a real streak.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m2-level-4a~ca0_2","q":"D7 retention for 2 users joined on Jan 3; one returns on Jan 10. Value and denominator?","a":"<p>50%: one of the two (cohort size is the denominator, not login rows), returning exactly on day 7.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m2-level-4a~ca0_3","q":"Why LEFT JOIN logins to the cohort?","a":"<p>To keep users who never returned; an inner join would drop them and inflate retention.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m2-level-4a~ta1_1","q":"<p>User 2 logged in on 3 January and then on 5, 6, 7, 8, 9 and 10 January. Apply the same trick: what labels do you get, and what is the longest streak?</p>\n","a":"<p>Row numbers 1 to 7. 3 Jan − 1 = 2 Jan. Then 5 Jan − 2 = 3 Jan, 6 − 3 = 3 Jan, and so on to 10 Jan − 7 = 3 Jan. Two groups: &quot;2 Jan&quot; (just 3 January, length 1) and &quot;3 Jan&quot; (5 to 10 January, length 6). The longest streak is 6 days.</p>\n"}]},"m2-level-4b":{"id":"m2-level-4b","slug":"sql-problems-level-4b-funnels-churn-windows-sessions","m":2,"title":"SQL problems, level 4b: funnels, churn windows, sessions","min":35,"type":"practice","priority":"Highest","prereq":["m2-level-4a"],"tags":["sql","practice","funnels","sessions"],"qa":9,"ai":{"scope":"Conversion funnels (simple and ordered), users who bought in one month but not the next, and sessionization with a 30-minute gap.","keyTerms":["funnel","conditional aggregation","ordered funnel","anti-join","sessionization","LAG","cumulative SUM"],"interviewQs":["Build a view to cart to purchase funnel. How do you enforce the order of steps?","Find users who purchased in January but not in February.","Split events into sessions with a 30-minute inactivity gap."],"mistakes":["Counting events instead of distinct users in a funnel","Not enforcing step order","Using timestamp BETWEEN with inclusive end dates"],"practice":"Take the sample events and compute both funnel versions, then sessionize user 4 by hand before running the query.","related":["m2-level-4a","m13-metric-drop"],"points":"Q: Funnel: view -> cart -> purchase conversion.\nA: Stricter version (ordered funnel: user must have done the steps in sequence) uses self-joins or MIN(CASE WHEN ... THEN event_time END) per step and checks ordering.\n\nQ: Users who bought in Jan but not in Feb.\nA: \n\nQ: Sessionization: new session if gap > 30 min.\nA: "},"intuitive":"<p>A funnel is a slide with three sections: lots of people look at a product, fewer add it to the cart, even fewer buy. You count how many different people reached each section. To make sure they did the steps in the right order, you check the time of each step. A session is one visit to an app: if someone goes quiet for more than 30 minutes, the next click starts a new visit. You mark &quot;new visit&quot; whenever the gap from the previous click is big, then add up the marks to number the visits.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Funnels and sessions are the bread and butter of product analytics SQL.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Count distinct users per step; enforce order with per-step first timestamps; sessionise with LAG plus a running SUM.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;A funnel counts distinct users at each step: viewers, carters, buyers, using COUNT DISTINCT with CASE WHEN. For a strict funnel I take each user&#39;s first timestamp per step and require view before cart before purchase, so someone who bought without carting does not count. For users who bought in January but not February, I use half-open date ranges and NOT EXISTS. For sessions, I use LAG to get the previous event time, flag a new session when there is none or the gap exceeds thirty minutes, and take a running SUM of the flags to number sessions. The catch: count users, not events, and enforce step order.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"A funnel, with and without step order","html":"<p>The events table has 11 clicks from 5 users. How many users got to each step?</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Count distinct users per step, not events.</strong> User 4 viewed three times but is one viewer.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>step</th><th>users</th><th>as % of viewers</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>view</td><td>5 (users 1 to 5)</td><td>100%</td></tr><tr><td>cart</td><td>2 (users 1, 2)</td><td>40%</td></tr><tr><td>purchase</td><td>2 (users 1, 5)</td><td>40%</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ol start=\"2\">\n<li><strong>Notice the oddity.</strong> Purchases equal carts, yet the buyers are not the same people. User 5 viewed and then purchased without a cart event.</li>\n<li><strong>Ask the clarifying question.</strong> &quot;Should a purchase count only if a cart came first?&quot; A strict funnel requires each step to follow the previous one.</li>\n<li><strong>Strict version.</strong> Take each user&#39;s first time at each step and require view &lt; cart &lt; purchase. Only user 1 qualifies: viewed 10:00, cart 10:05, purchased 10:20.</li>\n<li><strong>Report both.</strong> Loose funnel: 5 → 2 → 2. Strict funnel: 5 → 2 → 1. The gap tells the product team that some users buy without the cart, perhaps through a &quot;buy now&quot; button.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Funnel numbers depend on definitions: users versus events, and whether order is enforced. Say your definition before giving the numbers.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-p16-the-two-funnel-versions","text":"P16: the two funnel versions","level":3},{"id":"h-p17-bought-in-january-but-not-february","text":"P17: &quot;bought in January but not February&quot;","level":3},{"id":"h-p18-sessionization-step-by-step","text":"P18: sessionization, step by step","level":3},{"id":"h-more-problems-with-solutions","text":"More problems, with solutions","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"qa","id":"2.5#16","src":"c","q":"Funnel: view -&gt; cart -&gt; purchase conversion.","a":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">COUNT</span>(<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">DISTINCT</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">CASE</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHEN</span> event_name <span class=\"hljs-operator\">=</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;view&#x27;</span>     <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">THEN</span> user_id <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">END</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> viewers,\n  <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">COUNT</span>(<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">DISTINCT</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">CASE</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHEN</span> event_name <span class=\"hljs-operator\">=</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;cart&#x27;</span>     <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">THEN</span> user_id <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">END</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> carters,\n  <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">COUNT</span>(<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">DISTINCT</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">CASE</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHEN</span> event_name <span class=\"hljs-operator\">=</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;purchase&#x27;</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">THEN</span> user_id <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">END</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> buyers\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> events;</code></pre></div>\n<p>Stricter version (ordered funnel: user must have done the steps in sequence) uses self-joins or <code>MIN(CASE WHEN ... THEN event_time END)</code> per step and checks ordering.</p>\n","s":"Count distinct users per step with conditional aggregation; for an ordered funnel compare each user&#39;s first timestamp per step.","label":"P16"},{"k":"qa","id":"2.5#17","src":"c","q":"Users who bought in Jan but not in Feb.","a":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">DISTINCT</span> user_id <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> orders\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> order_date <span class=\"hljs-operator\">&gt;=</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-01-01&#x27;</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AND</span> order_date <span class=\"hljs-operator\">&lt;</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-02-01&#x27;</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AND</span> user_id <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">NOT</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">IN</span> (\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> user_id <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> orders <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> order_date <span class=\"hljs-operator\">&gt;=</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-02-01&#x27;</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AND</span> order_date <span class=\"hljs-operator\">&lt;</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-03-01&#x27;</span>);</code></pre></div>\n","s":"Users with a January order who are not in the set of February buyers (<code>NOT EXISTS</code>), using half-open date ranges.","label":"P17"},{"k":"qa","id":"2.5#18","src":"c","q":"Sessionization: new session if gap &gt; 30 min.","a":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WITH</span> flagged <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> (\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> user_id, event_time,\n         <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">CASE</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHEN</span> event_time <span class=\"hljs-operator\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">LAG</span>(event_time) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> user_id <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> event_time)\n                   <span class=\"hljs-operator\">&gt;</span> <span class=\"hljs-type\">INTERVAL</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;30 minutes&#x27;</span>\n              <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">OR</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">LAG</span>(event_time) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> user_id <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> event_time) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">IS</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">NULL</span>\n              <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">THEN</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ELSE</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">END</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> new_sess\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> events\n)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span>, <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">SUM</span>(new_sess) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> user_id <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> event_time) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> session_id\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> flagged;</code></pre></div>\n","s":"<code>LAG</code> gives the previous event; flag gaps above 30 minutes; a running <code>SUM</code> of flags numbers the sessions.","label":"P18"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-p16-the-two-funnel-versions\">P16: the two funnel versions</h3>\n<p>Simple (each step counted independently):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>viewers</th><th>carters</th><th>buyers</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>5</td><td>2</td><td>2</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Ordered funnel: each user must view, then add to cart, then purchase, using each user&#39;s first timestamp per step:</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WITH</span> f <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> (\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> user_id,\n         <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">MIN</span>(<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">CASE</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHEN</span> event_name <span class=\"hljs-operator\">=</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;view&#x27;</span>     <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">THEN</span> event_time <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">END</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> v,\n         <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">MIN</span>(<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">CASE</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHEN</span> event_name <span class=\"hljs-operator\">=</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;cart&#x27;</span>     <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">THEN</span> event_time <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">END</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> c,\n         <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">MIN</span>(<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">CASE</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHEN</span> event_name <span class=\"hljs-operator\">=</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;purchase&#x27;</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">THEN</span> event_time <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">END</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> p\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> events <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> user_id\n)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">COUNT</span>(<span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FILTER</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> v <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">IS</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">NOT NULL</span>)        <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> viewed,\n       <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">COUNT</span>(<span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FILTER</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> c <span class=\"hljs-operator\">&gt;</span> v)                <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> then_cart,\n       <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">COUNT</span>(<span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FILTER</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> p <span class=\"hljs-operator\">&gt;</span> c <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AND</span> c <span class=\"hljs-operator\">&gt;</span> v)      <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> then_purchase\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> f;</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>viewed</th><th>then_cart</th><th>then_purchase</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>5</td><td>2</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>User 5 purchased without adding to the cart, so the simple funnel counts them as a buyer but the ordered funnel (correctly) does not. NULL comparisons evaluate to unknown, so users missing a step are excluded automatically. In MySQL, replace <code>COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE x)</code> with <code>SUM(x)</code> or <code>COUNT(CASE WHEN x THEN 1 END)</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-p17-bought-in-january-but-not-february\">P17: &quot;bought in January but not February&quot;</h3>\n<p>Result: user 4. User 4 ordered in January (order 6) but not in February, while users 1, 2 and 3 ordered in both months. The date filters use half-open ranges (<code>&gt;= &#39;2025-01-01&#39; AND &lt; &#39;2025-02-01&#39;</code>). <code>NOT IN</code> is safe here because <code>user_id</code> in <code>orders</code> is never NULL, but <code>NOT EXISTS</code> is the habit to build.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-p18-sessionization-step-by-step\">P18: sessionization, step by step</h3>\n<p>For users 1 and 4 (user 4 has a 38-minute gap before the last event):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>user_id</th><th>event_time</th><th>new_sess</th><th>session_id</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>10:00</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>10:05</td><td>0</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>10:20</td><td>0</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>13:00</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>13:02</td><td>0</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>13:40</td><td>1</td><td>2</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Steps: (1) <code>LAG(event_time)</code> gets the previous event per user; (2) flag <code>new_sess = 1</code> when there is no previous event <strong>or</strong> the gap exceeds 30 minutes; (3) a running <code>SUM(new_sess)</code> per user turns the flags into session numbers 1, 2, 3.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-more-problems-with-solutions\">More problems, with solutions</h3>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m2-level-4b~1","q":"Show the funnel split by device.","a":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> u.device,\n       <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">COUNT</span>(<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">DISTINCT</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">CASE</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHEN</span> e.event_name <span class=\"hljs-operator\">=</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;view&#x27;</span>     <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">THEN</span> e.user_id <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">END</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> viewers,\n       <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">COUNT</span>(<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">DISTINCT</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">CASE</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHEN</span> e.event_name <span class=\"hljs-operator\">=</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;purchase&#x27;</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">THEN</span> e.user_id <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">END</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> buyers\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> events e <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">JOIN</span> users u <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">USING</span> (user_id)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> u.device;</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>device</th><th>viewers</th><th>buyers</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>android</td><td>2</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>ios</td><td>2</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>web</td><td>1</td><td>0</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Segmenting a funnel by device, country or app version is how you localise a conversion drop. Remember small counts make percentages noisy.</p>\n","s":"Join events to users and group by device, using <code>COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN ...)</code> per step."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m2-level-4b~2","q":"How many sessions does each user have, using a 30-minute inactivity rule?","a":"<p>Reuse the flag step and aggregate:</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WITH</span> flagged <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> (\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> user_id, event_time,\n         <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">CASE</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHEN</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">LAG</span>(event_time) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> user_id <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> event_time) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">IS</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">NULL</span>\n                <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">OR</span> event_time <span class=\"hljs-operator\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">LAG</span>(event_time) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> user_id <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> event_time) <span class=\"hljs-operator\">&gt;</span> <span class=\"hljs-type\">INTERVAL</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;30 minutes&#x27;</span>\n              <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">THEN</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ELSE</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">END</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> new_sess\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> events\n)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> user_id, <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">SUM</span>(new_sess) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> sessions <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> flagged <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> user_id;</code></pre></div>\n<p>Only user 4 has two sessions; the others have one. You can now compute sessions per user per day, average session length (<code>MAX - MIN</code> of time per session) and events per session.</p>\n","s":"Count the <code>new_sess</code> flags per user."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m2-level-4b~ca0_1","q":"Why can a simple funnel and an ordered funnel differ?","a":"<p>The simple version counts a user who purchased without a cart view as a buyer; the ordered version requires the steps in sequence.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m2-level-4b~ca0_2","q":"How do you number sessions with a 30-minute gap rule?","a":"<p>Flag rows where the gap from LAG(event_time) exceeds 30 minutes or is NULL, then <code>SUM(flag) OVER (PARTITION BY user ORDER BY time)</code>.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m2-level-4b~ca0_3","q":"Why prefer <code>&gt;= start AND &lt; next_start</code> to BETWEEN for dates?","a":"<p>BETWEEN includes only midnight of the end date for timestamps and would miss most of the last day.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m2-level-4b~ta1_1","q":"<p>User 4 has events at 13:00, 13:02 and 13:40. With a new session starting after a gap of more than 30 minutes, how many sessions does user 4 have? Show the gap column and the session number.</p>\n","a":"<p>Gaps from LAG: 13:00 → NULL (first event, so it starts session 1); 13:02 → 2 minutes (same session); 13:40 → 38 minutes, more than 30, so it starts a new one. Flag new sessions as 1, 0, 1. The running sum of the flags gives session numbers 1, 1, 2. User 4 has 2 sessions.</p>\n"}]},"m2-level-5a":{"id":"m2-level-5a","slug":"sql-problems-level-5a-median-pivot-share-of-total","m":2,"title":"SQL problems, level 5a: median, pivot, share of total","min":30,"type":"practice","priority":"Highest","prereq":["m2-level-3"],"tags":["sql","practice","analytics"],"qa":9,"ai":{"scope":"Analytics patterns: median by group, pivoting rows into columns with conditional aggregation, and percent-of-total with window functions.","keyTerms":["median","PERCENTILE_CONT","pivot","conditional aggregation","percent of total","window over aggregate"],"interviewQs":["Compute the median salary per department.","Pivot revenue by category into columns.","Show each category's percentage of total revenue."],"mistakes":["Using AVG when the median is asked","Hard-coding pivot columns without telling the interviewer they are static","Computing percent of total with an integer division"],"practice":"Compute the overall median order amount by hand from the sorted values, then write the portable SQL and check.","related":["m2-level-3","m2-level-5b"],"points":"Q: Median salary per department (Postgres).\nA: \n\nQ: Pivot: revenue by category per month as columns.\nA: \n\nQ: Percentage of total (share of revenue by category).\nA: "},"intuitive":"<p>The median is the middle person when everyone stands in a line by height, which is better than the average when one giant (or a billionaire) pulls the average up. A pivot rotates a table: instead of one row per category and month you get one column per category, like turning a list into a little spreadsheet. Percent of total asks &quot;how big is my slice of the pie?&quot;, which needs the size of the whole pie next to each slice.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Reporting questions (&quot;median delivery time&quot;, &quot;revenue by category per month&quot;, &quot;share of total&quot;) come up constantly.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Median via percentile or middle row numbers; pivot with SUM(CASE WHEN); share with SUM() OVER ().</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;The median is the middle value after sorting, and it resists outliers. In Postgres I use PERCENTILE_CONT of 0.5; portably I number rows, count rows, and average the middle one or two rows. A pivot turns rows into columns using conditional aggregation: SUM of CASE WHEN category equals X then revenue else zero, grouped by month. Percent of total divides each group&#39;s sum by the grand total using SUM of SUM over an empty window, with a decimal multiplier so it is not truncated. The catch: pivot columns are fixed in the query, so I mention that dynamic pivots need other tools.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Pivot revenue by category, traced","html":"<p>Turn &quot;one row per month and category&quot; into &quot;one row per month, one column per category&quot; (delivered orders, revenue from order items).</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Start long.</strong> The grouped data has one row per month and category:</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>month</th><th>category</th><th>revenue</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Jan</td><td>Electronics</td><td>7,000</td></tr><tr><td>Jan</td><td>Fashion</td><td>800</td></tr><tr><td>Feb</td><td>Fashion</td><td>3,900</td></tr><tr><td>Feb</td><td>Grocery</td><td>1,300</td></tr><tr><td>Mar</td><td>Electronics</td><td>4,000</td></tr><tr><td>Mar</td><td>Fashion</td><td>2,600</td></tr><tr><td>Mar</td><td>Grocery</td><td>1,500</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ol start=\"2\">\n<li><strong>Write one conditional sum per column.</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-built_in\">SUM</span>(<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">CASE</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHEN</span> category <span class=\"hljs-operator\">=</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Electronics&#x27;</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">THEN</span> revenue <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ELSE</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">END</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> electronics,\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">SUM</span>(<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">CASE</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHEN</span> category <span class=\"hljs-operator\">=</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Fashion&#x27;</span>     <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">THEN</span> revenue <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ELSE</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">END</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> fashion,\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">SUM</span>(<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">CASE</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHEN</span> category <span class=\"hljs-operator\">=</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Grocery&#x27;</span>     <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">THEN</span> revenue <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ELSE</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">END</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> grocery\n... <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">month</span></code></pre></div>\n<ol start=\"3\">\n<li><strong>How it works for January.</strong> The Electronics column adds 7,000 (the Electronics row) + 0 (the Fashion row) = 7,000.</li>\n<li><strong>The wide result.</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>month</th><th>electronics</th><th>fashion</th><th>grocery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Jan</td><td>7,000</td><td>800</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>Feb</td><td>0</td><td>3,900</td><td>1,300</td></tr><tr><td>Mar</td><td>4,000</td><td>2,600</td><td>1,500</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ol start=\"5\">\n<li><strong>Check the total:</strong> 7,800 + 5,200 + 8,100 = 21,100, the same as the delivered revenue by month. A pivot rearranges numbers; it must never change the total.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> <code>SUM(CASE WHEN ...)</code> is a pivot you can write in any database. With <code>ELSE 0</code>, a missing combination shows as 0 instead of NULL.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-p19-the-median-two-ways","text":"P19: the median, two ways","level":3},{"id":"h-p20-pivoting","text":"P20: pivoting","level":3},{"id":"h-p21-percent-of-total","text":"P21: percent of total","level":3},{"id":"h-more-problems-with-solutions","text":"More problems, with solutions","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"qa","id":"2.5#19","src":"c","q":"Median salary per department (Postgres).","a":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> dept_id, <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">PERCENTILE_CONT</span>(<span class=\"hljs-number\">0.5</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WITHIN</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">GROUP</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> salary) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> median_sal\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> employees <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> dept_id;\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\">-- Portable: use ROW_NUMBER and COUNT window, average the middle one or two rows.</span></code></pre></div>\n","s":"<code>PERCENTILE_CONT(0.5) WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY salary)</code>, or average the middle row(s) using <code>ROW_NUMBER</code> and <code>COUNT</code>.","label":"P19"},{"k":"qa","id":"2.5#20","src":"c","q":"Pivot: revenue by category per month as columns.","a":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> DATE_TRUNC(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;month&#x27;</span>, o.order_date) mth,\n       <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">SUM</span>(<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">CASE</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHEN</span> p.category <span class=\"hljs-operator\">=</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Electronics&#x27;</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">THEN</span> oi.qty<span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span>oi.price <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ELSE</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">END</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> electronics,\n       <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">SUM</span>(<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">CASE</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHEN</span> p.category <span class=\"hljs-operator\">=</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Fashion&#x27;</span>     <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">THEN</span> oi.qty<span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span>oi.price <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ELSE</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">END</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> fashion\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> orders o <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">JOIN</span> order_items oi <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">USING</span>(order_id) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">JOIN</span> products p <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">USING</span>(product_id)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>;</code></pre></div>\n","s":"Conditional aggregation: <code>SUM(CASE WHEN category = &#39;X&#39; THEN revenue ELSE 0 END)</code> per column, grouped by month.","label":"P20"},{"k":"qa","id":"2.5#21","src":"c","q":"Percentage of total (share of revenue by category).","a":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> category, <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">SUM</span>(rev) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> rev,\n       ROUND(<span class=\"hljs-number\">100.0</span> <span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">SUM</span>(rev) <span class=\"hljs-operator\">/</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">SUM</span>(<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">SUM</span>(rev)) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">OVER</span> (), <span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> pct_share\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> cat_revenue <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> category;</code></pre></div>\n","s":"<code>100.0 * SUM(rev) / SUM(SUM(rev)) OVER ()</code>: group total over the grand total.","label":"P21"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-p19-the-median-two-ways\">P19: the median, two ways</h3>\n<p>PostgreSQL gives it in one line:</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> dept_id, <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">PERCENTILE_CONT</span>(<span class=\"hljs-number\">0.5</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WITHIN</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">GROUP</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> salary) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> median_sal\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> employees <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> dept_id;</code></pre></div>\n<p>The portable version numbers the rows and averages the one or two middle rows:</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WITH</span> r <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> (\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> dept_id, salary,\n         <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">ROW_NUMBER</span>() <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> dept_id <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> salary) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> rn,\n         <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">COUNT</span>(<span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span>)     <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> dept_id)                  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> n\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> employees\n)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> dept_id, <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">AVG</span>(salary) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> median_sal\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> r\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> rn <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">IN</span> ((n <span class=\"hljs-operator\">+</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>) <span class=\"hljs-operator\">/</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>, (n <span class=\"hljs-operator\">+</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>) <span class=\"hljs-operator\">/</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>)      <span class=\"hljs-comment\">-- integer division: for odd n both equal the middle row, for even n they are the two middle rows</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> dept_id;</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>dept_id</th><th>median_sal</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>10</td><td>70000</td></tr><tr><td>20</td><td>60000</td></tr><tr><td>30</td><td>75000</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Department 30 has two people (55,000 and 95,000), so the median is their average, 75,000. <code>PERCENTILE_CONT</code> interpolates between the middle values; <code>PERCENTILE_DISC</code> returns an actual value from the data.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-p20-pivoting\">P20: pivoting</h3>\n<p>Result (one column per category, one row per month):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>month</th><th>electronics</th><th>fashion</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>2025-01</td><td>7000</td><td>800</td></tr><tr><td>2025-02</td><td>0</td><td>3900</td></tr><tr><td>2025-03</td><td>4000</td><td>2600</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><code>SUM(CASE WHEN category = &#39;Electronics&#39; THEN ... ELSE 0 END)</code> is called <strong>conditional aggregation</strong>. Standard SQL has no dynamic pivot: the columns must be listed in the query (some engines offer <code>PIVOT</code>; pandas&#39; <code>pivot_table</code> is the flexible alternative). Mention that limitation.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-p21-percent-of-total\">P21: percent of total</h3>\n<p>The inner <code>SUM(rev)</code> is the group total per category, the outer window <code>SUM(SUM(rev)) OVER ()</code> is the grand total repeated on each row:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>category</th><th>revenue</th><th>pct_share</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Electronics</td><td>11000</td><td>51.16</td></tr><tr><td>Fashion</td><td>7300</td><td>33.95</td></tr><tr><td>Grocery</td><td>3200</td><td>14.88</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Use <code>100.0 *</code> (not <code>100 *</code>) so the division is not truncated to an integer, and <code>ROUND</code> for presentation.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-more-problems-with-solutions\">More problems, with solutions</h3>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m2-level-5a~1","q":"What is the median order amount across all orders?","a":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WITH</span> r <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> (\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> amount, <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">ROW_NUMBER</span>() <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> amount) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> rn, <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">COUNT</span>(<span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">OVER</span> () <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> n <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> orders\n)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">AVG</span>(amount) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> median_amount <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> r <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> rn <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">IN</span> ((n <span class=\"hljs-operator\">+</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>) <span class=\"hljs-operator\">/</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>, (n <span class=\"hljs-operator\">+</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>) <span class=\"hljs-operator\">/</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>);</code></pre></div>\n<p>The 16 orders sorted are 300, 400, 500, 600, 700, 800, 900, 1100, 1200, 1400, ...; the 8th and 9th are 1,100 and 1,200, so the median is <strong>1,150</strong>, below the mean of 1,343.75 (a right-skewed distribution: a few big orders pull the mean up).</p>\n","s":"Number the orders by amount and average the middle one or two."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m2-level-5a~2","q":"Give each category&#39;s share of revenue within each month.","a":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WITH</span> m <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> (\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> DATE_TRUNC(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;month&#x27;</span>, o.order_date) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">month</span>, p.category, <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">SUM</span>(oi.qty <span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span> oi.price) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> rev\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> orders o <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">JOIN</span> order_items oi <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">USING</span> (order_id) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">JOIN</span> products p <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">USING</span> (product_id)\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>\n)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">month</span>, category, rev,\n       ROUND(<span class=\"hljs-number\">100.0</span> <span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span> rev <span class=\"hljs-operator\">/</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">SUM</span>(rev) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">month</span>), <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> pct_of_month\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> m;</code></pre></div>\n<p>For January that gives Electronics 85.4%, Fashion 9.8%, Grocery 4.9% (of 8,200). The <code>PARTITION BY month</code> makes the &quot;whole pie&quot; each month&#39;s own total.</p>\n","s":"Group by month and category, then divide by <code>SUM(SUM(rev)) OVER (PARTITION BY month)</code>."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m2-level-5a~ca0_1","q":"Sorted salaries 55k and 95k in one department. Median?","a":"<p>75k, the average of the two middle values.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m2-level-5a~ca0_2","q":"How do you write a pivot with one column per category?","a":"<p><code>SUM(CASE WHEN category = &#39;Fashion&#39; THEN revenue ELSE 0 END) AS fashion</code> per category, grouped by month.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m2-level-5a~ca0_3","q":"Why <code>100.0 * x / y</code> and not <code>100 * x / y</code>?","a":"<p>Integer division truncates; the decimal literal forces a decimal result.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m2-level-5a~ta1_1","q":"<p>The 16 order amounts sorted are: 300, 400, 500, 600, 700, 800, 900, 1,100, 1,200, 1,400, 1,500, 1,800, 2,200, 2,500, 2,600, 3,000. What is the median, and why might it be better than the mean (1,343.75) to describe a typical order?</p>\n","a":"<p>With 16 values the median is the average of the 8th and 9th: (1,100 + 1,200) / 2 = 1,150. The mean is higher because a few large orders (2,500, 2,600, 3,000) pull it up; the median shows what a typical order looks like and is not moved by those few. In SQL: <code>PERCENTILE_CONT(0.5) WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY amount)</code> in PostgreSQL, or number the rows and average the middle two elsewhere.</p>\n"}]},"m2-level-5b":{"id":"m2-level-5b","slug":"sql-problems-level-5b-churn-ntile-rfm-recursion","m":2,"title":"SQL problems, level 5b: churn, NTILE, RFM, recursion","min":35,"type":"practice","priority":"Highest","prereq":["m2-level-5a"],"tags":["sql","practice","analytics","recursion"],"qa":10,"ai":{"scope":"Churn candidates with an as-of date, quantile buckets with NTILE, RFM segmentation, recursive hierarchies, and all-periods-active patterns.","keyTerms":["churn","NTILE","RFM","recursive CTE","hierarchy","relational division","as-of date"],"interviewQs":["Find customers whose last order was more than 90 days ago.","Segment customers by recency, frequency and monetary value.","Use a recursive CTE to walk an employee hierarchy."],"mistakes":["Using CURRENT_DATE in a question that needs a fixed as-of date for reproducibility","Reading NTILE(5) as \"top 20%\" without checking ties and sizes","Forgetting the termination condition in recursion"],"practice":"Fix an as-of date of 2025-03-31 and recompute churn candidates, RFM scores and the org tree on the sample data.","related":["m2-level-5a","m13-metrics"],"points":"Q: Customers whose latest order was > 90 days ago (churn candidates).\nA: \n\nQ: Top 20% customers by spend (NTILE).\nA: \n\nQ: RFM segmentation (recency, frequency, monetary).\nA: \n\nQ: Recursive CTE: employee hierarchy.\nA: "},"intuitive":"<p>Churn candidates are customers who have gone quiet: their last visit was a long time ago. RFM scores every customer on three simple questions: how Recently did they buy, how Frequently, and how much Money did they spend? NTILE is a fair pile-maker: it sorts customers and deals them into equal-sized piles (top 20%, next 20%, and so on). A recursive query is like asking &quot;who works for my boss?&quot;, then &quot;who works for those people?&quot;, repeating until you reach the bottom of the company tree.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Marketing, CRM and org-structure questions use exactly these patterns.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Use an as-of date, NTILE for buckets, RFM as three NTILEs, and a recursive CTE with an anchor plus a step.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;Churn candidates are customers whose last order is older than a window, so I group by user, take the maximum order date, and compare with an as-of date, a fixed date rather than today so results are reproducible. NTILE deals ordered rows into equal buckets, but ties can straddle boundaries. RFM computes recency, frequency and monetary per customer then NTILE scores each. A recursive CTE has an anchor, such as the root employee, and a recursive step joining children to what we have, adding one to the level, until nothing new appears. &#39;Has done X in every month&#39; is a group with COUNT DISTINCT equal to the number of months. The catch: guard against cycles and be explicit about the as-of date.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Walk an org chart with a recursive query","html":"<p>Who reports to whom, and at what level? The practice data has Asha at the top.</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WITH</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">RECURSIVE</span> chain <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> (\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> emp_id, name, <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> lvl <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> employees <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> manager_id <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">IS</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">NULL</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">UNION</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ALL</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> e.emp_id, e.name, c.lvl <span class=\"hljs-operator\">+</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> employees e <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">JOIN</span> chain c <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ON</span> e.manager_id <span class=\"hljs-operator\">=</span> c.emp_id)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> chain;</code></pre></div>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Anchor (runs once):</strong> people with no manager. Result: Asha, level 1.</li>\n<li><strong>Round 1:</strong> join employees whose manager is in the current result (Asha). Adds Ravi, Meera, Isha and Omar at level 2.</li>\n<li><strong>Round 2:</strong> whose manager is among those four? Karan (Ravi), Dev and Nina (Isha), Zoya (Omar), at level 3.</li>\n<li><strong>Round 3:</strong> whose manager is among Karan, Dev, Nina or Zoya? Nobody. An empty round ends the recursion.</li>\n<li><strong>Result:</strong> 9 rows: 1 person at level 1, 4 at level 2, 4 at level 3.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> A recursive query is a loop written in SQL: start from the anchor, keep adding the next layer, and stop when a round adds nothing. If the data had a cycle, such as A managing B and B managing A, it would never stop, so production queries add a depth limit.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-p22-churn-candidates-need-an-as-of-date","text":"P22: churn candidates need an as-of date","level":3},{"id":"h-p23-and-p24-ntile-and-rfm","text":"P23 and P24: NTILE and RFM","level":3},{"id":"h-p25-the-recursive-cte-in-two-parts","text":"P25: the recursive CTE, in two parts","level":3},{"id":"h-more-problems-with-solutions","text":"More problems, with solutions","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"qa","id":"2.5#22","src":"c","q":"Customers whose latest order was &gt; 90 days ago (churn candidates).","a":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> user_id, <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">MAX</span>(order_date) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> last_order\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> orders <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> user_id\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">HAVING</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">MAX</span>(order_date) <span class=\"hljs-operator\">&lt;</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">CURRENT_DATE</span> <span class=\"hljs-operator\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-type\">INTERVAL</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;90 days&#x27;</span>;</code></pre></div>\n","s":"Group by user, keep those whose <code>MAX(order_date)</code> is older than an as-of date minus the window.","label":"P22"},{"k":"qa","id":"2.5#23","src":"c","q":"Top 20% customers by spend (NTILE).","a":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WITH</span> s <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> user_id, <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">SUM</span>(amount) spend <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> orders <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> user_id),\n     b <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span>, <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">NTILE</span>(<span class=\"hljs-number\">5</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> spend <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">DESC</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> quintile <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> s)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> b <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> quintile <span class=\"hljs-operator\">=</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>;</code></pre></div>\n","s":"<code>NTILE(5) OVER (ORDER BY spend DESC)</code> and keep bucket 1; ties can straddle boundaries.","label":"P23"},{"k":"qa","id":"2.5#24","src":"c","q":"RFM segmentation (recency, frequency, monetary).","a":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WITH</span> rfm <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> (\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> user_id,\n         <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">CURRENT_DATE</span> <span class=\"hljs-operator\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">MAX</span>(order_date) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> recency,\n         <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">COUNT</span>(<span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> frequency,\n         <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">SUM</span>(amount) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> monetary\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> orders <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> user_id\n)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span>,\n       <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">NTILE</span>(<span class=\"hljs-number\">4</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> recency <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ASC</span>)   <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> r_score,  <span class=\"hljs-comment\">-- low recency days = better</span>\n       <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">NTILE</span>(<span class=\"hljs-number\">4</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> frequency <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">DESC</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> f_score,\n       <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">NTILE</span>(<span class=\"hljs-number\">4</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> monetary <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">DESC</span>)  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> m_score\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> rfm;</code></pre></div>\n","s":"Compute recency, frequency and monetary per user, then <code>NTILE</code> each into scores.","label":"P24"},{"k":"qa","id":"2.5#25","src":"c","q":"Recursive CTE: employee hierarchy.","a":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WITH</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">RECURSIVE</span> org <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> (\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> emp_id, name, manager_id, <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> level <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> employees <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> manager_id <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">IS</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">NULL</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">UNION</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ALL</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> e.emp_id, e.name, e.manager_id, o.level <span class=\"hljs-operator\">+</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> employees e <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">JOIN</span> org o <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ON</span> e.manager_id <span class=\"hljs-operator\">=</span> o.emp_id\n)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> org;</code></pre></div>\n","s":"Anchor on the root (manager IS NULL), then repeatedly join children to the rows found so far, adding 1 to the level.","label":"P25"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-p22-churn-candidates-need-an-as-of-date\">P22: churn candidates need an as-of date</h3>\n<p><code>CURRENT_DATE</code> changes every day, so results are not reproducible. For learning, fix the date. With an as-of date of 2025-03-31 and a 30-day window (the sample data only spans three months), the customers whose last order was before 1 March are:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>user_id</th><th>last_order</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>3</td><td>2025-02-03</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>(Users 1, 2, 4, 5, 6, 7 have all ordered since; user 8 never ordered, so is not in <code>orders</code> at all; to include them, start from <code>users</code> with a <code>LEFT JOIN</code>.)</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-p23-and-p24-ntile-and-rfm\">P23 and P24: NTILE and RFM</h3>\n<p>NTILE(n) sorts the rows and deals them into n buckets of (almost) equal size; bucket sizes differ by at most one when the row count does not divide evenly. Important caveats: <strong>ties are split arbitrarily</strong> across bucket boundaries, and the labels are relative (the &quot;top 20%&quot; of a bad quarter is still relative to that quarter).</p>\n<p>Terciles of spend on the sample data:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>user_id</th><th>spend</th><th>tercile</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>4</td><td>5600</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>5100</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>3700</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>2900</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1600</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>1400</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>1200</td><td>3</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>RFM (as of 2025-03-31, with three buckets; lower r-score is better because recency counts days since the last order):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>user_id</th><th>recency (days)</th><th>frequency</th><th>monetary</th><th>r</th><th>f</th><th>m</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>29</td><td>4</td><td>5100</td><td>3</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>16</td><td>2</td><td>5600</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>13</td><td>2</td><td>1200</td><td>1</td><td>3</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>11</td><td>1</td><td>1400</td><td>1</td><td>3</td><td>3</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Interpretation: user 4 is a high-value, recent customer (1-1-1); user 1 buys often and spends a lot but has not bought for a month (3-1-1: a win-back target). Segments are built from the score triples.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-p25-the-recursive-cte-in-two-parts\">P25: the recursive CTE, in two parts</h3>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WITH</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">RECURSIVE</span> org <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> (\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> emp_id, name, manager_id, <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> level            <span class=\"hljs-comment\">-- anchor: the top of the tree</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> employees <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> manager_id <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">IS</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">NULL</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">UNION</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ALL</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> e.emp_id, e.name, e.manager_id, o.level <span class=\"hljs-operator\">+</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>     <span class=\"hljs-comment\">-- recursive step: join children to the rows found so far</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> employees e <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">JOIN</span> org o <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ON</span> e.manager_id <span class=\"hljs-operator\">=</span> o.emp_id\n)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> org;</code></pre></div>\n<p>It first takes Asha (level 1). Then each loop joins the employees whose manager is in the previous result: Ravi, Meera, Isha, Omar (level 2); then Karan, Dev, Nina, Zoya (level 3). It stops when a step returns no new rows. A cycle in the data would loop forever; most engines have a recursion limit, and you can guard with a depth condition.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-more-problems-with-solutions\">More problems, with solutions</h3>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m2-level-5b~1","q":"Show each employee&#39;s management chain as text (for example <code>Asha &gt; Omar &gt; Zoya</code>).","a":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WITH</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">RECURSIVE</span> org <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> (\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> emp_id, name, <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> level, name <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> path <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> employees <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> manager_id <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">IS</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">NULL</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">UNION</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ALL</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> e.emp_id, e.name, o.level <span class=\"hljs-operator\">+</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>, o.path <span class=\"hljs-operator\">||</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27; &gt; &#x27;</span> <span class=\"hljs-operator\">||</span> e.name\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> employees e <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">JOIN</span> org o <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ON</span> e.manager_id <span class=\"hljs-operator\">=</span> o.emp_id\n)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> name, level, path <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> org <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> path;</code></pre></div>\n<p>Rows include <code>Isha | 2 | Asha &gt; Isha</code>, <code>Dev | 3 | Asha &gt; Isha &gt; Dev</code> and <code>Zoya | 3 | Asha &gt; Omar &gt; Zoya</code>. Use <code>CONCAT</code> in MySQL. The same carry-a-value idea computes subtree totals or depth.</p>\n","s":"Carry a <code>path</code> string through the recursion, appending each employee&#39;s name."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m2-level-5b~2","q":"Find users who ordered in <strong>every</strong> month of the data (January, February and March).","a":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> user_id\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> orders\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> user_id\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">HAVING</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">COUNT</span>(<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">DISTINCT</span> DATE_TRUNC(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;month&#x27;</span>, order_date)) <span class=\"hljs-operator\">=</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>;</code></pre></div>\n<p>Users 1 and 2. The pattern &quot;has all of the required items&quot; is called relational division; the number on the right comes from <code>(SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT month) FROM orders)</code> when it is not fixed.</p>\n","s":"Group by user and require <code>COUNT(DISTINCT month) = 3</code> (relational division)."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m2-level-5b~ca0_1","q":"Why use a fixed as-of date instead of CURRENT_DATE?","a":"<p>Results stay reproducible and testable; CURRENT_DATE changes every day.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m2-level-5b~ca0_2","q":"Two parts of a recursive CTE?","a":"<p>An anchor query (the root rows) and a recursive step that joins the table to the CTE&#39;s previous output, combined with UNION ALL.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m2-level-5b~ca0_3","q":"Find users who ordered in all 3 months of data.","a":"<p><code>GROUP BY user_id HAVING COUNT(DISTINCT month) = 3</code> (relational division).</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m2-level-5b~ta1_1","q":"<p>&quot;Churn candidates&quot;: as of 31 March 2025, which users&#39; last delivered order is more than 30 days old? Last delivered orders: user 1 on 2 March, user 2 on 5 March, user 3 on 3 February, user 4 on 15 March, user 5 on 9 March, user 6 on 18 March, user 7 on 20 March.</p>\n","a":"<p>The cut-off is 1 March 2025 (31 March minus 30 days). Only user 3 (3 February) is earlier, so user 3 is the churn candidate. In SQL: <code>SELECT user_id FROM orders WHERE status = &#39;delivered&#39; GROUP BY user_id HAVING MAX(order_date) &lt; DATE &#39;2025-03-31&#39; - 30</code>. Use a fixed as-of date rather than <code>CURRENT_DATE</code> so the result does not change each day. User 8 has never ordered, which is a different question (anti-join).</p>\n"}]},"m2-concepts":{"id":"m2-concepts","slug":"conceptual-sql-questions","m":2,"title":"Conceptual SQL questions","min":25,"type":"learn","priority":"Highest","prereq":[],"tags":["sql","interview-questions"],"qa":19,"ai":{"scope":"Conceptual SQL questions: set operations, indexes, keys, deletion commands, normalisation, transactions, OLTP vs OLAP and query optimisation.","keyTerms":["UNION ALL","index","B-tree","clustered index","CTE vs temp table","DELETE vs TRUNCATE","normalisation","ACID","OLAP","EXPLAIN"],"interviewQs":["What is an index and what does it cost?","DELETE vs TRUNCATE vs DROP?","How do you optimise a slow query?","Explain ACID with an example."],"mistakes":["Saying indexes are free","Confusing clustered and non-clustered indexes","Answering optimisation without mentioning the query plan"],"practice":"Explain each row aloud in 30 seconds with one example, then run EXPLAIN on a query from the sample data.","related":["m2-checklist","m2-traps"],"points":"Q: WHERE vs HAVING?\nA: WHERE filters rows before grouping; HAVING filters groups after aggregation.\n\nQ: UNION vs UNION ALL?\nA: UNION removes duplicates (extra sort/hash cost); UNION ALL keeps all rows.\n\nQ: INNER vs LEFT join?\nA: INNER returns matches only; LEFT keeps all left rows, NULLs for non-matches.\n\nQ: RANK vs DENSE_RANK?\nA: RANK leaves gaps after ties; DENSE_RANK does not.\n\nQ: CTE vs subquery vs temp table?\nA: CTE is readable/reusable in one statement; temp table is materialized, can be indexed, persists in session.\n\nQ: What is an index?\nA: A data structure (usually B-tree) that speeds reads at the cost of writes and storage.\n\nQ: Clustered vs non-clustered?\nA: Clustered defines physical row order (one per table); non-clustered is a separate structure pointing to rows.\n\nQ: Primary vs unique key?\nA: Primary: unique + not null, one per table. Unique: allows one NULL (DB dependent), many per table.\n\nQ: DELETE vs TRUNCATE vs DROP?\nA: DELETE removes rows (filterable, logged, rollback); TRUNCATE removes all rows fast, resets identity; DROP removes the table.\n\nQ: Normalization?\nA: Organizing tables to reduce redundancy: 1NF atomic values, 2NF no partial dependency, 3NF no transitive dependency.\n\nQ: OLTP vs OLAP?\nA: OLTP: many small transactions, normalized. OLAP: analytical queries over large data, denormalized (star schema).\n\nQ: How to optimize a slow query?\nA: Check the EXPLAIN plan, add indexes on join/filter columns, avoid SELECT *, filter early, avoid functions on indexed columns, reduce joins fan-out, use partitioning.\n\nQ: COUNT(*) vs COUNT(col)?\nA: COUNT(*) counts rows; COUNT(col) counts non-NULL values.\n\nQ: What is a self join?\nA: Joining a table to itself, e.g., employee-manager.\n\nQ: What are ACID properties?\nA: Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, Durability."},"intuitive":"<p>An index is like the index at the back of a textbook: instead of reading every page to find &quot;photosynthesis&quot;, you jump straight to the right page. It makes finding things faster, but every time the book changes you must also update the index, so writing gets a little slower. A transaction is like transferring money: either both the subtraction and the addition happen, or neither does.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> These are the short &quot;explain it&quot; questions that follow every SQL coding round.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Give a one-line definition, a cost or trade-off, and a tiny example for each.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;An index is a sorted structure, usually a B-tree, that makes lookups and range scans fast at the cost of slower writes and more storage. A clustered index is the table&#39;s physical order, one per table; non-clustered are separate structures pointing to rows. DELETE removes chosen rows and can be rolled back, TRUNCATE empties the table quickly, DROP removes the table. UNION removes duplicates, UNION ALL keeps them and is faster. ACID means atomic, consistent, isolated, durable: a money transfer either fully happens or not at all. OLTP is many small transactions, OLAP is large analytical scans. To optimise a slow query I read the plan, index join and filter columns, filter early, and avoid functions on indexed columns. The catch: indexes are not free.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Why an index makes a lookup fast: count the reads","html":"<p>A table of 1,000,000 orders. Find the order with <code>order_id = 742,113</code>.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Without an index:</strong> the database reads rows until it finds the match. On average half the table, 500,000 rows; if the id is missing, all 1,000,000.</li>\n<li><strong>With a B-tree index:</strong> the ids are kept sorted in a tree. Each node holds about 100 keys and points to the right child for the next range.</li>\n<li><strong>Count the levels.</strong> 100 keys per node gives 100 entries after one level, 100² = 10,000 after two and 100³ = 1,000,000 after three. So <strong>3 node reads</strong> reach the right entry, plus 1 read to fetch the row.</li>\n<li><strong>Compare:</strong> about 4 reads against about 500,000. That is why a lookup on an indexed column feels instant.</li>\n<li><strong>The cost.</strong> Every insert or update must also update the index, and the index takes disk space. So index the columns you filter, join and sort on, not every column.</li>\n<li><strong>When the index cannot help.</strong> <code>WHERE YEAR(order_date) = 2025</code> applies a function to every row&#39;s date before comparing, so the sorted order of the index is useless. A range on the raw column, <code>order_date &gt;= &#39;2025-01-01&#39; AND order_date &lt; &#39;2026-01-01&#39;</code>, uses it.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> An index is a sorted shortcut, like the index at the back of a book. It makes reads fast and writes slower.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-answers-with-a-little-more-depth","text":"Answers with a little more depth","level":3},{"id":"h-how-to-answer-optimise-this-slow-query","text":"How to answer &quot;optimise this slow query&quot;","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"qa","id":"2.6#1","src":"c","q":"WHERE vs HAVING?","a":"<p>WHERE filters rows before grouping; HAVING filters groups after aggregation.</p>\n","s":"<code>WHERE</code> filters rows before grouping; <code>HAVING</code> filters groups after aggregation."},{"k":"qa","id":"2.6#2","src":"c","q":"UNION vs UNION ALL?","a":"<p>UNION removes duplicates (extra sort/hash cost); UNION ALL keeps all rows.</p>\n","s":"<code>UNION</code> removes duplicates (extra sort or hash cost); <code>UNION ALL</code> keeps every row and is faster."},{"k":"qa","id":"2.6#3","src":"c","q":"INNER vs LEFT join?","a":"<p>INNER returns matches only; LEFT keeps all left rows, NULLs for non-matches.</p>\n","s":"<code>INNER</code> keeps matches only; <code>LEFT</code> keeps every left row and fills NULLs where there is no match."},{"k":"qa","id":"2.6#4","src":"c","q":"RANK vs DENSE_RANK?","a":"<p>RANK leaves gaps after ties; DENSE_RANK does not.</p>\n","s":"<code>RANK</code> leaves gaps after ties (1, 1, 3); <code>DENSE_RANK</code> does not (1, 1, 2)."},{"k":"qa","id":"2.6#5","src":"c","q":"CTE vs subquery vs temp table?","a":"<p>CTE is readable/reusable in one statement; temp table is materialized, can be indexed, persists in session.</p>\n","s":"A CTE is a named subquery for readability within one statement; a temp table is materialised, indexable and reusable."},{"k":"qa","id":"2.6#6","src":"c","q":"What is an index?","a":"<p>A data structure (usually B-tree) that speeds reads at the cost of writes and storage.</p>\n","s":"A structure (usually a B-tree) that speeds reads at the cost of slower writes and more storage."},{"k":"qa","id":"2.6#7","src":"c","q":"Clustered vs non-clustered?","a":"<p>Clustered defines physical row order (one per table); non-clustered is a separate structure pointing to rows.</p>\n","s":"Clustered = the physical row order (one per table); non-clustered = a separate structure pointing at rows."},{"k":"qa","id":"2.6#8","src":"c","q":"Primary vs unique key?","a":"<p>Primary: unique + not null, one per table. Unique: allows one NULL (DB dependent), many per table.</p>\n","s":"Primary key: unique and not null, one per table; unique constraint: many per table, may allow NULL."},{"k":"qa","id":"2.6#9","src":"c","q":"DELETE vs TRUNCATE vs DROP?","a":"<p>DELETE removes rows (filterable, logged, rollback); TRUNCATE removes all rows fast, resets identity; DROP removes the table.</p>\n","s":"<code>DELETE</code> removes chosen rows (logged, rollback-able); <code>TRUNCATE</code> empties the table fast; <code>DROP</code> removes the table."},{"k":"qa","id":"2.6#10","src":"c","q":"Normalization?","a":"<p>Organizing tables to reduce redundancy: 1NF atomic values, 2NF no partial dependency, 3NF no transitive dependency.</p>\n","s":"Organising tables to reduce redundancy: 1NF atomic values, 2NF no partial dependency, 3NF no transitive dependency."},{"k":"qa","id":"2.6#11","src":"c","q":"OLTP vs OLAP?","a":"<p>OLTP: many small transactions, normalized. OLAP: analytical queries over large data, denormalized (star schema).</p>\n","s":"OLTP: many small transactions on normalised data; OLAP: big analytical queries on denormalised (star) schemas."},{"k":"qa","id":"2.6#12","src":"c","q":"How to optimize a slow query?","a":"<p>Check the EXPLAIN plan, add indexes on join/filter columns, avoid <code>SELECT *</code>, filter early, avoid functions on indexed columns, reduce joins fan-out, use partitioning.</p>\n","s":"Read the query plan, index join/filter columns, avoid <code>SELECT *</code>, filter early, avoid functions on indexed columns."},{"k":"qa","id":"2.6#13","src":"c","q":"COUNT(*) vs COUNT(col)?","a":"<p>COUNT(*) counts rows; COUNT(col) counts non-NULL values.</p>\n","s":"<code>COUNT(*)</code> counts rows; <code>COUNT(col)</code> counts non-NULL values."},{"k":"qa","id":"2.6#14","src":"c","q":"What is a self join?","a":"<p>Joining a table to itself, e.g., employee-manager.</p>\n","s":"Joining a table to itself, for example employee to manager."},{"k":"qa","id":"2.6#15","src":"c","q":"What are ACID properties?","a":"<p>Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, Durability.</p>\n","s":"Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, Durability: the guarantees of a reliable transaction."},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-answers-with-a-little-more-depth\">Answers with a little more depth</h3>\n<p><strong>Index, clustered vs non-clustered.</strong> A B-tree index keeps keys sorted so equality and range lookups take O(log n) instead of scanning the whole table. The cost: extra storage and slower <code>INSERT</code>/<code>UPDATE</code>/<code>DELETE</code> (the index must be maintained). A <strong>clustered</strong> index <em>is</em> the table&#39;s physical order (only one per table; the primary key by default in SQL Server and MySQL InnoDB). A <strong>non-clustered</strong> (secondary) index is a separate structure whose entries point back to the rows. Composite indexes follow the <strong>leftmost prefix</strong> rule: an index on <code>(a, b)</code> helps filters on <code>a</code> or <code>a, b</code>, but not on <code>b</code> alone.</p>\n<p><strong>CTE vs subquery vs temp table.</strong> A CTE is a named subquery for readability within one statement (some engines inline it, some materialise it). A subquery in <code>FROM</code> is similar but anonymous. A temp table is materialised for the session, can be indexed, and is reusable across statements, which helps when an expensive intermediate result is used many times.</p>\n<p><strong>DELETE vs TRUNCATE vs DROP.</strong> <code>DELETE</code> removes chosen rows (supports <code>WHERE</code>, fires triggers, fully logged, can be rolled back). <code>TRUNCATE</code> removes all rows quickly and resets identity counters (minimal logging; in some engines it cannot be rolled back). <code>DROP</code> removes the table definition too.</p>\n<p><strong>Primary vs unique key.</strong> A primary key is unique and non-null, one per table. A unique constraint allows NULLs (how many depends on the engine) and there can be many.</p>\n<p><strong>Normalisation.</strong> 1NF: atomic values, no repeating groups. 2NF: every non-key column depends on the whole key (no partial dependency). 3NF: no non-key column depends on another non-key column (no transitive dependency). Analytics schemas deliberately denormalise into <strong>star schemas</strong>: a central fact table (orders) with dimension tables (users, products, dates).</p>\n<p><strong>ACID, with an example.</strong> Transferring ₹500 from A to B: <em>Atomicity</em> (both the debit and the credit happen or neither), <em>Consistency</em> (total money is preserved and constraints hold), <em>Isolation</em> (another transaction does not see a half-done transfer), <em>Durability</em> (after commit, a crash cannot undo it).</p>\n<p><strong>OLTP vs OLAP.</strong> OLTP: many small reads and writes, normalised, row-oriented (PostgreSQL serving an app). OLAP: fewer, larger scans and aggregations, denormalised, often column-oriented (BigQuery, Snowflake, Redshift).</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-how-to-answer-optimise-this-slow-query\">How to answer &quot;optimise this slow query&quot;</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Look at the plan: <code>EXPLAIN</code> (or <code>EXPLAIN ANALYZE</code>). Find the expensive step (full scan, big sort, huge join).</li>\n<li>Add or fix <strong>indexes</strong> on join and filter columns; make filters <strong>sargable</strong> (write <code>order_date &gt;= &#39;2025-01-01&#39;</code>, not <code>YEAR(order_date) = 2025</code>, which blocks index use).</li>\n<li><strong>Select only needed columns</strong> (avoid <code>SELECT *</code>) and <strong>filter early</strong> (push <code>WHERE</code> before joins).</li>\n<li>Watch for <strong>join fan-out</strong> and aggregate before joining when possible.</li>\n<li>Consider partitioning big tables by date, pre-aggregated summary tables, and materialised views.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">EXPLAIN <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> orders <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> user_id <span class=\"hljs-operator\">=</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>;      <span class=\"hljs-comment\">-- PostgreSQL; in SQLite use EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">CREATE</span> INDEX idx_orders_user_date <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ON</span> orders (user_id, order_date);</code></pre></div>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m2-concepts~ca0_1","q":"Why can <code>WHERE YEAR(order_date) = 2025</code> be slow even with an index on order_date?","a":"<p>The function on the column prevents index use (not sargable); use a range: <code>order_date &gt;= &#39;2025-01-01&#39; AND order_date &lt; &#39;2026-01-01&#39;</code>.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m2-concepts~ca0_2","q":"Explain ACID with a bank transfer.","a":"<p>Atomic: debit and credit both happen or neither. Consistent: total money preserved. Isolated: others never see half the transfer. Durable: once committed it survives a crash.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m2-concepts~ca0_3","q":"When would you pick a temp table over a CTE?","a":"<p>When an expensive intermediate result is reused across statements or needs an index.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m2-concepts~ta1_1","q":"<p>There is an index on <code>orders(user_id)</code>. Which of these can use it, and why? (a) <code>WHERE user_id = 42</code>; (b) <code>WHERE user_id + 0 = 42</code>; (c) <code>WHERE user_id IN (3, 7, 9)</code>; (d) <code>WHERE CAST(user_id AS TEXT) = &#39;42&#39;</code>.</p>\n","a":"<p>(a) Yes: a direct comparison on the indexed column. (c) Yes: three quick lookups. (b) and (d) No (in most databases): the expression changes the column before comparing, so the database cannot use the sorted index and scans every row. The rule is to keep the indexed column alone on one side of the comparison, which is called being &quot;sargable&quot;.</p>\n"}]},"m2-traps":{"id":"m2-traps","slug":"common-sql-traps-interviewers-watch-for","m":2,"title":"Common SQL traps interviewers watch for","min":20,"type":"learn","priority":"Highest","prereq":["m2-level-2"],"tags":["sql","interview-questions","pitfalls"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"Seven common SQL mistakes that interviewers watch for, each demonstrated on the sample data with the wrong and right results.","keyTerms":["fan-out","NOT IN NULL","LEFT JOIN WHERE","integer division","BETWEEN timestamps","ties","DISTINCT users"],"interviewQs":["Why did my sum double after a join?","Why does my LEFT JOIN behave like an INNER JOIN?","Why is my percentage 0?"],"mistakes":["Joining before aggregating","Forgetting DISTINCT when counting users","Putting right-table filters in WHERE on a LEFT JOIN"],"practice":"For each trap, write a query that exhibits the bug and another that fixes it on the sample data.","related":["m2-level-2","m2-checklist"],"points":"1. Joining before aggregating, causing row fan-out and inflated sums.\n2. Using NOT IN with NULLs.\n3. Forgetting to DISTINCT users when counting unique users.\n4. Filtering on the right table of a LEFT JOIN in WHERE (turns it into an INNER join). Put the condition in ON instead.\n5. Integer division: 100 * a / b with integers truncates; cast to float or multiply by 1.0.\n6. Date boundaries: use >= start AND < next_day instead of BETWEEN for timestamps.\n7. Ties in ranking: choose RANK/DENSE_RANK/ROW_NUMBER deliberately and state why."},"intuitive":"<p>Most wrong SQL answers are not wrong because of difficult ideas; they are wrong because of small traps: counting the same order several times after a join, treating &quot;unknown&quot; as if it were a value, dividing two whole numbers and getting zero, or filtering in the wrong place. Learn the seven traps below and you will catch most mistakes before the interviewer does.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Interviewers deliberately set data where these traps bite, then watch whether you notice.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Check the grain before joining, respect NULLs, cast before dividing, and put conditions where they belong.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;Most wrong SQL answers come from seven traps. Row fan-out: joining a one-to-many table before summing inflates totals. NOT IN with NULLs returns nothing. Counting rows instead of distinct users. Putting a right-table filter in WHERE after a left join turns it into an inner join; the condition belongs in ON. Integer division truncating percentages. Using BETWEEN on timestamps. And ties in ranking functions. In the sample data fan-out gave 28,800 instead of 21,500. The catch: all of these produce plausible numbers, so I sanity check row counts and totals after every join and say my assumptions aloud.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"The LEFT JOIN that silently became an INNER JOIN","html":"<p>Goal: every user from 6 to 8, with their orders over 600 if they have any.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Version A puts the condition in WHERE.</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> u.user_id, o.amount <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> users u\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">LEFT</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">JOIN</span> orders o <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ON</span> o.user_id <span class=\"hljs-operator\">=</span> u.user_id\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> u.user_id <span class=\"hljs-operator\">&gt;=</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">6</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AND</span> o.amount <span class=\"hljs-operator\">&gt;</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">600</span>;</code></pre></div>\n<ol start=\"2\">\n<li><strong>Trace it.</strong> The join produces (6, 700), (6, 500), (7, 1,400) and (8, NULL). Now WHERE runs: 500 fails. <code>NULL &gt; 600</code> is unknown, so user 8&#39;s row fails too. Result: (6, 700) and (7, 1,400). <strong>User 8 has vanished.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Version B moves the condition into ON.</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> u.user_id, o.amount <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> users u\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">LEFT</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">JOIN</span> orders o <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ON</span> o.user_id <span class=\"hljs-operator\">=</span> u.user_id <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AND</span> o.amount <span class=\"hljs-operator\">&gt;</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">600</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> u.user_id <span class=\"hljs-operator\">&gt;=</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">6</span>;</code></pre></div>\n<ol start=\"4\">\n<li><strong>Trace it.</strong> The join only matches orders over 600, and the LEFT JOIN still keeps every user: (6, 700), (7, 1,400) and (8, NULL). User 8 stays, with nothing matched.</li>\n<li><strong>The rule.</strong> Conditions on the <em>right-hand</em> table of a LEFT JOIN go in ON. Conditions on the left-hand table (here <code>u.user_id &gt;= 6</code>) can stay in WHERE.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Both queries run without errors and both results look plausible. Only tracing the NULL row reveals that one of them answers a different question.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-1-row-fan-out","text":"1. Row fan-out","level":3},{"id":"h-2-not-in-with-nulls","text":"2. NOT IN with NULLs","level":3},{"id":"h-3-forgetting-distinct-when-counting-users","text":"3. Forgetting DISTINCT when counting users","level":3},{"id":"h-4-filtering-the-right-table-of-a-left-join-in-wh","text":"4. Filtering the right table of a LEFT JOIN in WHERE","level":3},{"id":"h-5-integer-division","text":"5. Integer division","level":3},{"id":"h-6-date-boundaries-and-between","text":"6. Date boundaries and BETWEEN","level":3},{"id":"h-7-ties-in-ranking","text":"7. Ties in ranking","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<ol>\n<li>Joining before aggregating, causing <strong>row fan-out</strong> and inflated sums.</li>\n<li>Using <code>NOT IN</code> with NULLs.</li>\n<li>Forgetting to <code>DISTINCT</code> users when counting unique users.</li>\n<li>Filtering on the right table of a LEFT JOIN in <code>WHERE</code> (turns it into an INNER join). Put the condition in <code>ON</code> instead.</li>\n<li>Integer division: <code>100 * a / b</code> with integers truncates; cast to float or multiply by <code>1.0</code>.</li>\n<li>Date boundaries: use <code>&gt;= start AND &lt; next_day</code> instead of <code>BETWEEN</code> for timestamps.</li>\n<li>Ties in ranking: choose RANK/DENSE_RANK/ROW_NUMBER deliberately and state why.</li>\n</ol>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-1-row-fan-out\">1. Row fan-out</h3>\n<p><code>orders</code> has one row per order; <code>order_items</code> has one row per item. After the join, each order repeats once per item.</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">SUM</span>(o.amount) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> orders o <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">JOIN</span> order_items oi <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">USING</span> (order_id);   <span class=\"hljs-comment\">-- 28,800  WRONG</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">SUM</span>(amount)   <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> orders;                                           <span class=\"hljs-comment\">-- 21,500  right</span></code></pre></div>\n<p>Fix: aggregate each table to the target grain before joining, or only sum item-level columns (<code>qty * price</code>). Sanity check: compare the row counts before and after a join (here 16 orders become 19 rows).</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-2-not-in-with-nulls\">2. <code>NOT IN</code> with NULLs</h3>\n<p>Shown in the checklist: one NULL in the subquery makes <code>NOT IN</code> return nothing. Use <code>NOT EXISTS</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-3-forgetting-distinct-when-counting-users\">3. Forgetting <code>DISTINCT</code> when counting users</h3>\n<p>The <code>logins</code> table has 21 rows but only 20 distinct user-days (user 1 logged in twice on 3 January). <code>COUNT(*)</code> overcounts activity; <code>COUNT(DISTINCT user_id)</code> counts people. State which one the question wants (&quot;events&quot; or &quot;users&quot;).</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-4-filtering-the-right-table-of-a-left-join-in-wh\">4. Filtering the right table of a LEFT JOIN in <code>WHERE</code></h3>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> u.user_id, o.order_id\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> users u <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">LEFT</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">JOIN</span> orders o <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ON</span> o.user_id <span class=\"hljs-operator\">=</span> u.user_id\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> o.status <span class=\"hljs-operator\">=</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;delivered&#x27;</span>;                         <span class=\"hljs-comment\">-- user 8 disappears: NULL = &#x27;delivered&#x27; is unknown</span></code></pre></div>\n<p>The <code>WHERE</code> runs after the join, so rows where <code>o</code> is all NULL are removed, turning the LEFT JOIN into an INNER JOIN. Move the condition into <code>ON</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">... <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">LEFT</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">JOIN</span> orders o <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ON</span> o.user_id <span class=\"hljs-operator\">=</span> u.user_id <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AND</span> o.status <span class=\"hljs-operator\">=</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;delivered&#x27;</span></code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>user_id</th><th>order_id (condition in ON)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>3</td><td>5</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>NULL</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>(User 3&#39;s cancelled order 4 no longer matches, leaving only order 5; user 8 stays with a NULL.)</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-5-integer-division\">5. Integer division</h3>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">100</span> <span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span> <span class=\"hljs-operator\">/</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">8</span>;       <span class=\"hljs-comment\">-- 37   (integer division truncates; PostgreSQL, SQL Server)</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">100.0</span> <span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span> <span class=\"hljs-operator\">/</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">8</span>;     <span class=\"hljs-comment\">-- 37.5</span></code></pre></div>\n<p>Multiply by <code>1.0</code> or cast one side to a decimal/float before dividing. MySQL&#39;s <code>/</code> returns a decimal, but do not rely on it across engines.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-6-date-boundaries-and-between\">6. Date boundaries and <code>BETWEEN</code></h3>\n<p><code>BETWEEN &#39;2025-01-01&#39; AND &#39;2025-01-31&#39;</code> includes midnight of the 31st only: a timestamp at <code>2025-01-31 15:00</code> is excluded. Use <code>&gt;= &#39;2025-01-01&#39; AND &lt; &#39;2025-02-01&#39;</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-7-ties-in-ranking\">7. Ties in ranking</h3>\n<p><code>ROW_NUMBER</code> with ties gives arbitrary but distinct numbers; <code>RANK</code> and <code>DENSE_RANK</code> give equal numbers. Decide deliberately, add a tie-breaker when you need determinism, and say why.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m2-traps~1","q":"&quot;Count the customers who ordered in March.&quot; A candidate writes <code>SELECT COUNT(*) FROM orders o JOIN order_items oi USING (order_id) WHERE order_date &gt;= &#39;2025-03-01&#39;</code>. What is wrong?","a":"<p>Two problems: (1) the join to <code>order_items</code> fans out the rows, and (2) <code>COUNT(*)</code> counts rows rather than distinct customers. The question only needs <code>orders</code>: <code>SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT user_id) FROM orders WHERE order_date &gt;= &#39;2025-03-01&#39; AND order_date &lt; &#39;2025-04-01&#39;</code>, which returns 6 for March (users 1, 2, 4, 5, 6, 7). Also clarify whether cancelled orders count.</p>\n","s":"It counts item rows, not customers; count <code>DISTINCT user_id</code> and avoid the unnecessary join."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m2-traps~ca0_1","q":"After <code>users LEFT JOIN orders ... WHERE o.status = &#39;delivered&#39;</code>, users with no orders vanish. Why and fix?","a":"<p>NULL = &#39;delivered&#39; is unknown, so those rows are filtered, making it an inner join. Put the condition in the ON clause.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m2-traps~ca0_2","q":"<code>SELECT 100 * 3 / 8</code> returns 37 in Postgres. Why?","a":"<p>Integer division truncates. Use <code>100.0 * 3 / 8</code> for 37.5.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m2-traps~ca0_3","q":"&quot;Customers who ordered in March&quot;: which two mistakes does a join to order_items plus COUNT(*) make?","a":"<p>It fans out to item rows and counts rows rather than distinct customers; use COUNT(DISTINCT user_id) on orders alone.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m2-traps~ta1_1","q":"<p>15 of the 16 orders are delivered. In PostgreSQL, what do <code>SELECT 100 * 15 / 16</code> and <code>SELECT 100.0 * 15 / 16</code> return, and which one belongs in a report?</p>\n","a":"<p><code>100 * 15 / 16</code> is all integers, so the division truncates: 1,500 / 16 = 93. <code>100.0 * 15 / 16</code> is decimal: 93.75. The report needs 93.75%. The same trap appears as <code>COUNT(...) / COUNT(*)</code>, which returns 0 for any share below 100% when both counts are integers. Multiply by 1.0 or 100.0 first.</p>\n"}]},"m2-practice":{"id":"m2-practice","slug":"practice-platforms-and-self-check","m":2,"title":"Practice platforms and self-check","min":10,"type":"revise","priority":"Highest","prereq":[],"tags":["sql","practice","plan"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"How to practise SQL effectively, a script for live SQL rounds, and the self-check for Module 2.","keyTerms":["timed SQL practice","SQL interview script","pattern list","self-check"],"interviewQs":["Walk me through how you approach a new SQL question.","How do you validate your query is correct?"],"mistakes":["Writing the full query before clarifying the grain and the output","Not testing with a tiny example","Practising only easy problems"],"practice":"Run a 60-minute timed mock: two medium problems and one hard problem, using a fresh dataset.","related":["m2-level-4a","m2-traps"],"points":"Platform - Target\nLeetCode SQL 50 (study plan) - Complete all\nDataLemur - 40+ problems incl. hard\nStrataScratch - 30+ from Indian product companies (Amazon, Uber, etc.)\nHackerRank SQL - Advanced certificate\nMode SQL tutorial - Window functions section\n\n- Can you write the retention query (P14) and streak query (P13) from scratch?\n- Can you explain why a LEFT JOIN with a WHERE on the right table becomes an INNER JOIN?\n- Can you solve a medium problem in 15 minutes and a hard problem in 25?"},"intuitive":"<p>SQL interview questions come from about ten patterns. Missing rows: LEFT JOIN with IS NULL. Best per group: rank inside each group. Running totals: SUM over ordered rows. Compared to last time: LAG. Streaks: date minus row number. Retention and funnels: count distinct users per step. Pivot and share of total: conditional sums and window totals. Org trees: recursive queries. Learn these and new questions look like old friends. In the interview, say what each row means, plan in steps, and test on two or three rows by hand.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> SQL is the biggest single filter, and calm structure impresses as much as the query.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Ten patterns, a step-by-step script, and a hand check on a tiny example.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;For a live SQL round I restate the question and the output grain, such as one row per user. I inspect the tables for keys, NULLs, duplicates and date types, and ask about ties and cancelled orders. I plan in steps aloud: a CTE for this, rank that, filter. I write it incrementally, then check on two or three rows by hand and confirm no fan-out by comparing row counts. Finally I mention performance, like indexes and filtering early. The patterns I rely on are anti-join, rank per group, running totals, LAG, gaps and islands, cohort and funnel counts, pivots and recursion. The catch: never write the whole query before agreeing on the definition.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"From question to query in five moves","html":"<p>Question: <em>&quot;For each city, which user has the highest total delivered spend?&quot;</em></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Output grain:</strong> one row per city, with the top user and their spend.</li>\n<li><strong>Inputs and grain:</strong> <code>users</code> (one row per user, has <code>city</code>) and <code>orders</code> (one row per order, has <code>amount</code> and <code>status</code>). The join is user to orders, one to many, which is fine because we sum order amounts at order grain.</li>\n<li><strong>Plan in steps aloud:</strong> (a) total delivered spend per user, with their city; (b) rank users within each city; (c) keep rank 1.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WITH</span> spend <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> (\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> u.city, u.user_id, <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">SUM</span>(o.amount) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> total\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> users u <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">JOIN</span> orders o <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ON</span> o.user_id <span class=\"hljs-operator\">=</span> u.user_id\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> o.status <span class=\"hljs-operator\">=</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;delivered&#x27;</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> u.city, u.user_id),\nranked <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> (\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span>, <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">RANK</span>() <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> city <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> total <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">DESC</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> r\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> spend)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> city, user_id, total <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> ranked <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> r <span class=\"hljs-operator\">=</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>;</code></pre></div>\n<ol start=\"4\">\n<li><strong>Check by hand.</strong> Bengaluru: user 4 has 5,600 against user 1&#39;s 5,100, so user 4. Mumbai: user 2 (3,700) beats user 7 (1,400). Pune: only user 5 (2,900). Delhi: users 3 and 6 both have 1,200, a tie.</li>\n<li><strong>Decide the tie, and say so.</strong> RANK returns both Delhi users (5 rows in total). If the business wants one per city, use ROW_NUMBER with a tie-breaker, for example the earlier sign-up.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Grain, plan, write, check by hand, decide the ties. The hand check found the tie, which a query that merely runs would never reveal.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"c-2-8","text":"2.8 Practice platforms and targets","level":2},{"id":"c-2-9","text":"2.9 Self-check (Module 2)","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-the-script-for-a-live-sql-round","text":"The script for a live SQL round","level":3},{"id":"h-pattern-list-the-vocabulary-of-sql-interviews","text":"Pattern list (the vocabulary of SQL interviews)","level":3},{"id":"h-practice-platforms-used-well","text":"Practice platforms, used well","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<h2 class=\"chunk-h\" id=\"c-2-8\"><span class=\"sec-num\">2.8</span> Practice platforms and targets</h2>"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Platform</th><th>Target</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>LeetCode SQL 50 (study plan)</td><td>Complete all</td></tr><tr><td>DataLemur</td><td>40+ problems incl. hard</td></tr><tr><td>StrataScratch</td><td>30+ from Indian product companies (Amazon, Uber, etc.)</td></tr><tr><td>HackerRank SQL</td><td>Advanced certificate</td></tr><tr><td>Mode SQL tutorial</td><td>Window functions section</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<h2 class=\"chunk-h\" id=\"c-2-9\"><span class=\"sec-num\">2.9</span> Self-check (Module 2)</h2>"},{"k":"cl","id":"m2-practice.c1","kind":"self","items":["Can you write the retention query (P14) and streak query (P13) from scratch?","Can you explain why a LEFT JOIN with a WHERE on the right table becomes an INNER JOIN?","Can you solve a medium problem in 15 minutes and a hard problem in 25?"]},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-the-script-for-a-live-sql-round\">The script for a live SQL round</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Restate</strong> what is asked and what the output columns and grain should be (&quot;one row per user, with their first order date&quot;).</li>\n<li><strong>Inspect the tables</strong>: keys, grain, NULLs, duplicates, date types. Ask about edge cases (ties? cancelled orders? time zones?).</li>\n<li><strong>Plan in steps</strong> out loud: &quot;first a CTE for X, then rank, then filter&quot;.</li>\n<li><strong>Write it step by step</strong>, running or eyeballing each CTE, naming things well.</li>\n<li><strong>Verify</strong> on two or three rows by hand; check that the row count and totals make sense (no fan-out).</li>\n<li><strong>Discuss</strong> performance (indexes, filtering early) and alternatives (window vs self-join).</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"h-pattern-list-the-vocabulary-of-sql-interviews\">Pattern list (the vocabulary of SQL interviews)</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Pattern</th><th>Tools</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Missing / not in another table</td><td>anti-join: <code>LEFT JOIN ... IS NULL</code>, <code>NOT EXISTS</code></td></tr><tr><td>Top N per group, latest per user</td><td><code>ROW_NUMBER</code> / <code>DENSE_RANK</code> + outer filter</td></tr><tr><td>Running totals, moving averages</td><td><code>SUM() OVER (ORDER BY ...)</code>, frames</td></tr><tr><td>Change versus previous</td><td><code>LAG</code> / <code>LEAD</code></td></tr><tr><td>Consecutive days / streaks</td><td>date minus <code>ROW_NUMBER</code> (gaps and islands)</td></tr><tr><td>Retention and cohorts</td><td>first-activity CTE + conditional <code>COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN ...)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Funnels and sessions</td><td>per-step timestamps, <code>LAG</code> gap flags, running <code>SUM</code></td></tr><tr><td>Pivot / share of total</td><td>conditional aggregation, <code>SUM() OVER ()</code></td></tr><tr><td>Hierarchies</td><td>recursive CTE</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Has all of&quot;</td><td><code>GROUP BY ... HAVING COUNT(DISTINCT ...) = n</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"h-practice-platforms-used-well\">Practice platforms, used well</h3>\n<p>LeetCode SQL 50 (breadth), DataLemur and StrataScratch (company-style questions), HackerRank SQL (for the online assessment format), Mode&#39;s window-function tutorial (concepts). Target from the course: finish the SQL 50 plan, 40+ DataLemur problems including hard ones, and 30+ StrataScratch problems from product companies.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m2-practice~1","q":"The interviewer says &quot;write a query for the retention rate&quot; with no other detail. What do you ask before writing SQL?","a":"<p>Clarify: (1) <strong>Definition</strong>: day-N retention (exactly day N), rolling retention, or monthly? (2) <strong>Cohort</strong>: by signup date, first purchase, or first app open? (3) <strong>Activity</strong>: what counts as &quot;active&quot; (any login, a purchase)? (4) <strong>Denominator</strong>: all users in the cohort? (5) <strong>Time window and granularity</strong>: which dates, day/week/month? (6) <strong>Edge cases</strong>: duplicates, test accounts, time zones. Then restate the definition, agree it, and write the query. Asking these questions is itself part of the signal.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m2-practice~ca0_1","q":"The interviewer says &quot;write the retention rate&quot;. What do you ask first?","a":"<p>Which definition (day-N, rolling, monthly), cohort basis, what counts as active, denominator, window and edge cases such as duplicates and test accounts.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m2-practice~ca0_2","q":"Name the pattern: &quot;users who never bought in the last 90 days but bought before&quot;.","a":"<p>Anti-join on a recent window (NOT EXISTS) combined with a prior-purchase condition, relative to an as-of date.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m2-practice~ca0_3","q":"How do you validate your query is right?","a":"<p>Compute a tiny case by hand, check row counts and totals reconcile, and test edge cases like ties and NULLs.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m2-practice~ta1_1","q":"<p>Name the pattern and outline the steps (no full SQL needed) for: <em>&quot;For each month, what share of orders came from customers who had ordered before?&quot;</em></p>\n","a":"<p>Pattern: first-order flag, then conditional aggregation by month. Steps: (1) for each order, find the customer&#39;s first order date, with <code>MIN(order_date) OVER (PARTITION BY user_id)</code> or a CTE; (2) flag the order as repeat if its date is after that first date; (3) group by month and compute <code>AVG(CASE WHEN is_repeat THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END)</code>; (4) check one month by hand and decide whether cancelled orders count. State the definition of &quot;repeat&quot; before writing anything.</p>\n"}]}});